In an emergency call, the professor confessed that he shot his girlfriend
During a call to an emergency line with a trembling voice, Professor Shannon Lamb confessed to police that he had shot his girlfriend and it is necessary for them to come to their home.
Lamb decided that his "sweet dog" was home alive and probably agitated, and that contacts on the dead woman's family could be found on her phone.
Inside the police found Amy Prentiss' body and a handwritten message on white office paper: "I'm so sorry and I wish I could return it, I loved Amy and she's the only one who's ever loved me," the letter signed by Lamb said.
There was no suspicion that Lamb, who taught two online courses for Delta State University in Cleveland, Mississippi, would drive 300 miles to a school campus where police believe he shot favorite Professor Ethan Schmidt at the door of his office.
Delta State University Police Chief Lynn Bufford said the university's staff heard shootings at 10:18 a.m.
Lamb is said to have made a fateful phone call to an emergency line shortly afterwards.
In the end, there was another death; Lamb took his own life when the police came for him.
The day after shooting at the school forced students and faculty to hide behind locked doors, authorized authorities are still trying to find out what Lamb's motivation was.
The details published by investigators on the part of the state, as well as students and employees who knew him, together created an image of a talented but probably troubled teacher.
The students confirmed they were looking forward to his lessons.
Police in Gautier, where Prentiss died, confirmed they had no criminal record or violent past.
Schmidt himself included Lambo in the book he had written in his thanks to the "great people" with whom he shared his academic life.
They both taught at the Faculty of Social Sciences and History along with 17 other faculty members, and many students went to classes in both at once.
At the same time, there have been inclinations when it comes to problems.
The student who praised Lamba, Brandon Beavers, said he also seemed agitated and shaky, "as if something was wrong with him."
Another student, Mikel Sykes, said Lamb told him he was grappling with stress at the end of the 2014-15 academic year.
Lamb has previously asked Delta State University for leave for health reasons, stating that it had a problem.
And this year he taught only two online courses.
Recent changes to the university's staff recruiting meant that a doctorate on which Lamb worked hard wouldn't guarantee him an automatic term to become an assistant professor.
The university's president, William LaForge, said he didn't know of any conflict between Lamb and Schmidt, but that "Mr. Lamm was apparently troubled by something."
Lamb won't answer those questions again.
After he drove off the campus, police intercepted his trail as he crossed the border of Arkansas back to Misissippi.
Before he may have been arrested, Lamb killed himself with a single-shot from a pistol in the yard of a house about a mile from his parents on the outskirts of Greenville, Mississippi, Washington state's coroner, Methel Johnson, confirmed.
He left the car started on the street.
It wasn't immediately clear why Lamb was going to this house, although Johnson said he probably knew the people who lived here.
Lamb grew up in this county.
Lamb began working at the university, which has 3,500 students in a city of about 12,000 residents, in 2009 and taught geography and education lessons.
In the spring, he received a doctorate in education.
One of Lamb's longtime friends described him as a clever, charismatic and funny person.
Carla Hairston said she was 15 and Lamb 20 when they met through mutual friends.
She was in high school with his friends, and he was the great older kid who had been teaching her guitar for several years.
It was quite a heartbreaker back then.
The hearts of all the girls started out as soon as it appeared," claims Hairston, who is now 40 years old and lives in a Jackson suburb.
Lamb and Prentiss reportedly dated for some time.
During an emergency phone call, Lamb said, "I killed my wife," but there was never a record of the wedding.
They had a dog named Lightning, who lived with them in a brick house adjacent to Gautier's arm.
Police said the dog was fine.
Prentiss's ex-husband, Shawn O'Steen, said they broke up 15 years ago, but they remained friends and had a daughter who is now 19 years old.
"She was completely collapsed," O'Steen said of his daughter.
She's a best friend with her mother.
O'Steen said he didn't meet Lamb, but through his daughter Abigail, Lamb was interested in music and played blues.
Lamb met Prentiss when he and Abigail performed at a summer festival three years ago.
They later played random gigs together.
O'Steen said his daughter writes and sings his own music.
Prentiss was a nurse who worked for various online companies.
Mike Shaffer, a bartender and occasional entertainment coordinator at The Julep Room, a sparely lit bar near Gautier, said he last saw Lamb and Prentiss on the night of September 5, the sweat when Lamb finished his guitar and harmonica for a small crowd.
There was no sign of anything bad.
"He was just a happy couple," he said.
We used to lash and record each other for a joke.
I mean, they both had a good sense of humor.
Schmidt, a slain professor, led a first-year seminar and specialized in original American and colonial history, said Don Allan Mitchell, an English professor at school.
He was married and had three young children.
He studied at Emporia State University in Kansas and was president of a boys' student club and student government.
On the Cleveland Delta State campus on Tuesday night, about 900 people, including faculty members, staff, students, and community members, took part in a commemorative event with candles.
Schmidt's wife Liz, and brother Jeff Schnidt also took part in a vigil during which the college choir sang "Bright Morning Star" and "Amazing Grace."
The school began again on Wednesday.
"We're trying to get our students to come back," LaForge argues.
The crisis is behind us.
It's a day of healing.
Amy and Rogelio Solis filed a report in Cleveland, Mississippi.
Writers for the Associated Press, Emily Wagster Pettus in Greenville, Mississippi, and Rebecca Santana in New Orleans have also contributed to this report.
This story has been edited to reflect the fact that police are now claiming that Lamb made a phone call to an emergency hotline after killing Schmidt, and that although Lamb called Prentiss his wife, there is no evidence that they were actually married.
Toys R Us plans to hire fewer seasonal workers
Toys R Us say they won't hire as many seasonal workers as they did last year, but children's toy and product makers say it will give current employees and seasonal workers the chance to work more hours.
The company also said it plans to hire about 40,000 employees to work in stores and distribution centers across the country, a decrease from 45,000 last season.
Most jobs will be part-time.
The company declares that it will start recruiting candidates this month, and will recruit employees during October until December.
While holidays on their own are months away, the shopping holiday season is approaching and companies are preparing to temporarily hire employees to help in stores and sales, for shipping and delivering goods.
Michael J. Fox jokes about his 'date' with Princess Diana
"Back to the future" premiere was a huge professional moment for Michael J. Fox.
It was also notable for personal reasons.
"We sat in the theater and waited for the arrival of the royal family when I realized that the seat next to me was empty and I realized Princess Diana was going to be sitting there," he said in a trailer for the upcoming documentary, "Backed back in time."
The film begins, and I realize that I'm within reach of something that could be called a false date with the Princess of Wales.
"Back in Time," is a documentary about "Returning to the Future," which conducts interviews with Fox, Lea Thompson and Christopher Lloyd, as well as director Robert Zemeckis and executive producer Steven Spielberg.
"Back for the Future" premiere was 30 years ago.
Spielberg said: "It's really unquestionably the best travel documentary that's ever been filmed."
"Back in time" will be available on VOD, DVD and in selected cinemas in October.
U.N. chief says there's no military solution in Syria
Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon says his response to Russia's accelerated military support for Syria is that "there is no military solution" to the conflict of the nearly five-year conflict, and more weapons will only exacerbate the violence and suffering of millions of people.
Once again, the U.N. chief urged all parties, including the divided UN Security Council, to unify and promote inclusive negotiations to find a political solution.
Ban said at Wednesday's press conference that he plans to meet with the foreign ministers of five permanent radios of countries - the U.S., Russia, China, Britain and France - on the sidelines of the ministerial meeting of the General Assembly at the end of this month to discuss the situation in Syria.
He expressed regret that divisions in the council and between the Syrian people and regional powers "have made this situation insoluble."
Ban urged five permanent members to show solidarity and unity, which they showed in reaching an Iranian nuclear deal in resolving the crisis in Syria.
8 electoral numbers that show Donald Trump in real light
Some tried to label him as a person who often changed opinions.
The others waved their hands like a failed joke.
And some are preparing for implosion.
But whether some Republicans are trying to pull Donald Trump down from the top of the election, they're not succeeding (yet).
10 out of the last 11 national polls show that Donald Trump is in the lead by a double-digit number, and some people are starting to seriously ask what this means for this tycoon's nomination chances from real estate.
Of course, it's just the beginning of the election cycle.
None of this says Trump is likely to win a Republican nomination.
Experts point out that at this time of 2011, Rick Perry's management made the path to the rise of Herman Cain, in the end, neither of them won a single position in the nominating process.
There are many reasons why he could come across in a general election.
But external groups such as Jeb Bush's Super PAC and economic conservative group Club for Growth are aware of Trump's will to endurance and are beginning to interpret their dollars to stand in order to overthrow it.
Here are some recent voting numbers that suggest that a real estate tycoon isn't just an ephemeral phase:
Trump's assessment of favority has turned 180 degrees.
Just before Donald Trump announced his candidacy in mid-June, Monmouth University's vote showed that only two out of 10 Republicans have a positive view of this real estate tycoon.
By mid-July it was already 40 percent.
At the beginning of August it was 52 percent.
Now, six out of 10 Republicans have a favorable view of Donald Trump.
Roughly three out of 10 say they have a negative opinion of him.
And these numbers are held in the early stages.
After the Quinnipiac University vote last week, 60 percent of Republicans were found to have a positive opinion of Trump.
Two-thirds of GOP electorates would be happy with Trump as a nominee.
In a CNN/ORC poll conducted last week, 67 percent of Republicans said they would be either "excited" or "satisfied" with Trump as a nominee.
Only two out of 10 said they would be "outraged" from his candidacy.
Only Ben Carson creates roughly the same level of enthusiasm as Trump (43 percent of people said they would be "enthusiastic" against 40 percent who say the same about Trump).
The next one in the level of enthusiasm?
Marco Rubio with only 21%.
On the other hand, 47 percent of Republican voters say they would be "dissatisfied" or "upset" if Jeb Bush became a candidate.
Most Republicans don't see Trump's temperament as a problem.
While Donald Trump has been widely criticized for his pompousiness and insults, 52 percent of Republican voters are nationally inclined to the idea that the real estate mogul has the right temperament for the post of president, according to Monday's ABC News/Washington Post.
The same number sticks to Iowa's senatorial club, which is the first in the entire country where 52 percent of Republicans think they have a commander's personality, according to Quinnipiac last week.
Still, 44 percent don't think he has a personality to serve effectively, and nearly 6 out of 10 independents say his temperament doesn't belong in the White House, according to ABC/Post.
Republican voters are getting used to this idea.
When he puts on his scholarly hats, Republican voters think Trump is a real-life nominee.
When asked who is most likely to win the GOP nominee, four out of 10 said Trump would be the best choice, according to last week's CNN/ORC poll.
That's a change from late July, when four out of 10 bet their money on Jeb Bush.
Full disclosure: GOP voters didn't have the brightest crystal ball in the past.
By this time in the previous cycle, 4 out of 10 Republicans chose Rick Perry as a candidacy winner against 28 percent for Mitt Romney's eventual nomination.
Still, it turns out that a certain number of GOP voters see Trump's campaign as plausible.
Even if Republicans rally around a different nominee, Trump beats almost everyone anyway.
Some scholars stress that a fragmented field is likely to contribute to Trump's leadership, while anti-Trump support is diffusely disseminated among more than a dozen other candidates.
But a Monmouth University poll in early September shows that in a hypothetical direct contest between Trump and most other Republican nominees, Trump will almost always get majority support.
He's 13 points above Carly Fiorina, Marc Rubie by 14 points, Walker by 15 points, Jeb Bush by 19 points and, finally, Rand Paul, John Kasich, Chris Christie by 33 points each.
He's in a dead race with Ted Cruz.
The only candidate who beats him?
Ben Carson would lead over the trader by a wide 19 points in a hypothetical matchup of one against one.
A barren majority of Donald Trump's supporters say they've already made up their minds.
Tuesday's CBS/NYT poll shows that only more than half of Trump-backed voters say they won't change their mind.
It's obvious that a lot of things can change it, and no one can really say he's never changed his mind.
46 percent say they're keeping an open backdoor for a change among the candidates.
Still, Trump's current strongest adversary is his outsider fellow neurosurger Ben Carson, but voters, who consider their opinion to be final, would go twice as likely to vote for Trump.
6 out of 10 Republicans reportedly agree with Trump on immigration issues.
Even since Donald Trump called immigrants from Mexion "rapists" in his campaign's announcement speech, immigration still sticks as the main and central theme of conversation for 2016.
Some are worried that Trump's pompousness will wick key Hispanic voters away from the Republican Party and the damage efforts of rebranding.
But according to Monday's new ABC/Post opinion poll, six out of 10 Republicans say they agree with Trump on immigration issues.
It seems that as long as the topic of immigration remains in the spotlight, Donald Trump will remain too.
Frustration from the government is reaching new highs.
Donald Trump and Ben Carson are now receiving roughly half the support from Republican voters, primarily due to their state of outsiders.
Six out of 10 Republicans in Monday's ABC/Post vote say they'd rather want a political outsider than someone with government experience.
And they're very angry with Washington, too.
Des Moines Register/Bloomberg in Iowa a few weeks ago revealed that three out of four Republicans in Iowa were frustrated by Republicans in Congress, 54 per cent were "dissatisfied" and 21 percent "helly angry."
The House backed a larger share of the state from coal charges.
The Czech Republic will receive more than 400 million.
At first reading, the Chamber of Deputies backed the government's amendment to the Mining Act, which regulates the amount of the proceeds of fees on surface brown coal mining from mining companies.
Minister Jan Mládek (ČSSD) promised MPs that thanks to this they would receive extra money for both the treasury and municipal budgets.
The draft was criticized by members of the opposition, who are worried that mining firms will no longer want to sponsor municipalities as before, because they will pay higher fees directly to the state budget.The House at first reading supported the government's amendment to the mining bill, which means, among other things, that the state should receive a larger part of the proceeds from the proceeds of brown coal mining fees from mining companies.
The state's share will increase to 67 percent, while the share of municipalities will decrease from 75 percent to 33 percent so far.
The draft also changes the method of calculating the payment for minerals that companies extract.
The change in fees for mining will bring 427 million crowns more to the treasury than in 2013.
According to the government, it should have a neutral impact on the revenue of municipal budgets, because the fee is supposed to be higher.
Last year, municipalities received about 450 million crowns from fees and the state budget received 150 million crowns.
About 200 million crowns went to the municipalities and 66 million crowns to the state of brown coal mining.
The amendment also foresees an increase in the payment from the mining areas with allowed mining to 1000 crowns per hectare per year.
The income from these payments should continue to belong to municipalities in its entirety.
Industry Minister Jan Mládek (ČSSD) told deputies that municipalities will receive 14 million to 100 million crowns a year instead of 14 million.
The amount of mining fees will no longer be determined by law, but stipulated by the government by its regulation.
Reimbursement is newly calculated as the product of the amount of extracted minerals and rates for individual types of minerals.
According to the government, the reason for the change is, among other things, the possibility of a flexible response to the current economic situation.
However, ODS MP Marek Benda warned that setting a fee may not comply with the constitution.
However, according to Minister Mládek, it is not a tax in this case, because the state charges the mineral, which is state property.
Fees are given only to municipalities whose territories are affected by mining.
In the case of deep-sea coal mining, three-quarters of the municipality's money and a quarter will be given to the
There will be the same ratio in the case of radioactive rock extraction.
A part of the money that the state collects on mining fees will be used on purpose.
For example, 28 percent will have to go to repair damage after mining or to provide mining emergency services.
Opposition MPs criticised the bill, arguing in particular that it would harm municipalities affected by mining and regions.
For example, Michal Kučera (TOP 09 and Mayors) has warned that mining companies will no longer want to sponsor municipalities as before, because they will pay higher fees directly to the state budget.
"It is not possible to tunnel one of the poorest regions just so that the Minister of Finance improves his budget," said David Kádner (Úsvit), elected in the Ústí region.
KSČM MP Hana Aulická warned against the increase in unemployment in the Most region.
Kádner and Aulická unsuccessfully proposed rejecting the amendment, Kučera wanted to return the law to the government for finalization.
According to mining firms, the increase in fees will not be reflected in coal prices, as prices are more than 90 percent long-term contracts.
Therefore, major consumers such as power plants or heating plants should not feel the increase in prices.
"However, on the other hand, this fact increases the risk that mining organizations will compensate for the increase in reimbursements by trying to reduce their operating expenses, which would primarily mean a reduction in the number of employees," the government admits.
According to her, however, the increase in fees in terms of employment is tolerable.
According to the rapporteur on the amendment by Milan Urban (ČSSD), it is crucial for MPs to know the conclusions about the updated energy concept and, above all, the government's decisions on coal mining limits before passing the law.
If coal is mined beyond the limits, it will have an impact on yields for the state and municipalities, he stressed.
Urban disagrees with Mládek that the decision to break the limit at the mine of the Czechoslovak army should fall only after 2020.
According to him, the limits on the CSA mine should be broken already because of social, national economic and because of the long-term stability of domestic energy.
