Jeremy Corbyn will make his debut at Prime Minister's Questions
Since his election, Mr Corbyn's debut at PMQ is very enthusiastically awaited
New manager Jeremy Corbyn will make his debut at the Prime Minister's Questions later, and for the first time he will work with David Cameron.
Mr Corbyn will ask the first of his six assigned questions shortly after noon, while his performance is likely to be carefully examined by the media and MPs.
In a week-long statement, he demanded "less theatre and more facts."
He also said he could skip several sessions he would leave to his colleagues.
This meeting will be the first parliamentary test by Mr Corby's leadership, which comes after his appointment to the shadow cabinet and his Tuesday speech to the TUC's annual congress.
In the meantime, the Labour leader's decision to stand still while singing the national anthem in service on Tuesday to mark the 75th anniversary of the Battle of Britain met with criticism from a number of Conservative MPs and is the focus of several stories on the newspaper's front pages.
Mr Corbyn's decision not to sing the national anthem drew attention
A spokesperson for Mr Corbyn said he was going to "stay in a respectful silence" and that he would recognize "the heroism of the Royal Air Force in the Battle of Britain."
However, a member of Mr Corbyn's shadow cabinet, Owen Smith, told the BBC programme Two's Newsnight that he would advise the Labour leader to sing the national anthem "regardless of" his belief that the monarchy should be abolished.
Almost a dozen shadow ministers refused to serve Mr Corbyn's top team, quoting differences in economics, defense and foreign affairs, and only less than one-sixth of the parliamentary party originally supported him as a leader.
BBC political correspondent Robin Brant says political differences in work are also being "aligned" after Mr Corbyn's appointment through his position in the European Union and the government's benefit limit.
Mr Corbyl said at the TUC conference that the Workers' Party had put forward amended proposals to remove the entire idea of the benefit limit.
Hours later, Mr. Smith, secretary of shadow work and pensions, said the party "very clearly" opposed the government's plans to reduce the limit from ₣26,000 to £23,000.
Mr. Corbyn will be the 5th leader of the Workers' Party faced by David Cameron over the past decade since his election as a Conservative leader.
The leader of the Workers' Party, who promised a different approach to politics, argues that he has ideas "out from the crowd" on questions for Mr Cameron and has received more than 30,000 proposals.
MP Islington North argues that PMQs are too confrontational and that they will refrain from both "prompted responses" and toothed trade, and instead promises to focus on important issues such as poverty, inequality and the challenges faced by young people.
Mr Corbyn argues that Angela Eagle, the shadow business secretary, will represent him at PMQ if she doesn't attend — such as when Mr Cameron travels abroad.
He also abandoned the idea that he would let other colleagues speak for him from time to time, because he was told that he had discussed the matter with House Speaker John Bercow.
When Mr Cameron became a leader in 2005, he said he wanted to move away from "Punch and Judy" politics, which was often associated with PMQ, but admitted a few years later that he had failed.
Since its first broadcast in 1990, PMQ has been seen as a key barometer of leaders' judgement, their leadership of the House, and their stance among other MPs, although critics have argued that they have become a cartoon and far-reaching reforms are needed.
"Photo shoot in Joburg": Young homeless trained as photographers
The center of Johannesburg is a difficult place for homelessness.
But one group of former street children found a way to learn something and make a living.
"I've been photographed in Joburg" is a non-profit studio that teaches young homeless people how to take photos of their neighborhood and make money.
BBC News met with one of the project's first graduates.
