The pound is rising, while salary growth in the UK is accelerating
The pound jumped in the following data and showed the recovery of Britain's wage growth, while the FTSE 100 was led higher using the SABMiller bid target.
Between May and June, GB gains including bonuses rose 2.9% compared with the same period last year.
This was the fastest since 2009, and it looked like estimates were approaching when rates would rise.
Sterling climbed more than 1% against the dollar to $1.5505 and was 0.84% higher against the euro at €1.3730.
FTSE 100 index closed at 1.4% to 6.229.21.
SABMiller was an exceptional stock on the market and jumped 20% after getting an approach from his rival AB InBev.
The deal would combine the world's two-largest breweries, and brand approaches such as Budweiser, Stella Artois, Peroni and Grolsch.
Burberry's shares were 0.9% higher after encouraging news from rival luxury goods group Richemont.
Richemont's latest sale results are above all expectations and showing that sales in China, a key market for Burberry, have started to rise again.
Shares at Glencore rise by 5% after the mining giant said they had risen by $2.5bn using shared placement as part of a debt-reduction strategy.
Shares in packaging and paper group Mondi were the biggest slump on the index and fell 4.3%, after Goldman Sachs downgraded his credit rating to the firm for "sale."
JD Sports rose 3.1% after a sports firm announced an 83% jump in mid-year in pre-tax profits of £46.6m
JD Sports boss says higher wages could hurt expansion
JD Sports' chief executive, Peter Cowgill, says a higher minimum wage for UK workers could mean "greater purchasing power in the pockets of potential consumers."
But it is unlikely that purchasing power would outweigh the higher wage costs at his firm, he states.
The cost could hit plans to expand JD Sports, he added, which could mean fewer new jobs.
Opponents of the marshmallown apartment in Prague are cheering.
Controversial construction got a shank.
Investor Praga Progetti e Investimenti considers the annulment of the decision to be unlawful and will appeal against it.
Hundreds of people protested against the building, which is supposed to be built in close proximity to the Agnes Monastery in the center of Prague.
In the decision, the municipality stated that there was a violation of the Building Act because not all the requirements of the zoning decision were complied with.
"The zoning decision does not contain certain requirements, for example, the type of land, the distance from the neighbouring buildings, a sufficiently clearly and understandably described shape of the building, the demarcation of the territory affected by the influence of the building," reads its decision.
It is also considered illegal by the municipality that some of the documents for the decision were issued for the originally discussed documentation, which, however, changed.
This includes, for example, the opinion of environmental authorities.
A part of the building is to be built on the grounds of the National Gallery, but the investor does not have its valid consent.
It follows from the file that the landowner, however, only gave his consent to the project documentation for zoning proceedings, not the consent to the location of the building on his lands, writes the Department of the Building Regulations.
According to Francesco August Razett of the investor firm, the Department of the Building Code did not take note of the decision of the superior authority - the Ministry of Regional Development, according to which the deadline for the initiation of the review has already expired.
In any case, this is a scandalous course of action.
"We have been preparing this project for seventeen years, complying with all the laws of the Czech Republic and respecting all authorities and institutions concerned," Razetto said in a press release.
The project, which because of the supposedly proposed color of the façade is nicknamed marshmaloun, provokes strong emotions in Prague.
Hundreds of people have gathered in the city centre in the past in protest against construction.
Associations and residents of nearby houses are annoyed that some permits were issued many years ago for a house that was supposed to look different.
They also criticize the unsuitability of the building for the historical center of Prague and the immediate neighbourhood of the Agnes Monastery.
The house was designed by the architect Zdeněk Fránek, the project changed several times.
Based on the opinion of the National Heritage Institute (NPÚ), the Ministry of Culture rejected the original form of the 2008 project, which counted on the glazed façade.
The new project of Praga Progetti e Investimenti with face-shaped windows was approved by the NPÚ and magistrate conservationists, and on this basis a building permit was issued.
The author of the proposal rejects the criticisms of opponents, saying that he never counted on a facade in a pink color, which gave the project a nickname after pink-and-white confectionery.
A Czech politician drove migrants.
Hungarians put him in jail with them.
Berg went to Hungary in order to bring humanitarian aid to refugees.
On the way, along with the crew, he took three Syrian refugees into the car, which he is said to have wanted to carry for about half a kilometre.
According to him, it was not yet criminal in Hungary at that time, he told the Lidové noviny newspaper.
We knew that the guardians of the law did not affect such conduct.
But when the officers stopped us, to our surprise, they arrested us and transferred us to pre-trial detention in Szeged.
In the end, the politician spent nine hours in the cell.
The officers were said to have behaved correctly, but there was no communication, because they did not speak English very well.
The prison, however, according to him, was sad.
For example, in our cell measuring about three times four meters, 15 refugees from Syria crowded.
Among them was a two-year-old boy or a four-year-old girl.
These people were locked up there the next day, and because of the language barrier, none of them knew what to do with them, Berg said.
What he can't confiscate anymore?
Ask the President of the Chamber of Executors.
On Friday 18.9. will answer the reader's questions from 9:30 a.m. the president of the Chamber of Executors of the Czech Republic Mgr. Pavla Fučíková.
You can ask her about changes to the Execution Act in force from September 1, 2015 and other things related to foreclosures or executors.
Metcash is launching a campaign in the countryside to fight Aldi's invasion
Metcash are trying to protect their IGA stores from Aldi's onslaught in southern and western Australia.
Metcash is launching a campaign in the countryside to fight Aldi's invasion
While Aldi prepares to open two distribution centers and the first of more than 120 stores in southern and western Australia early next year, Metcash and IGA supermarkets are strengthening relationships with local food suppliers and building the IGA brand using marketing in mainstream and social media and events such as the Adelaide show.
IGA traders and suppliers are added with brands such as Dunsborough-based Simmo's ice ice cream maker offering tastings at IGA stores, and store owners are selling discounted tickets to the Australian Football League to local customers.
Metcash is expected to release further details of its defence strategy on September 29, when food, alcohol and hardware retailers celebrate their annual investor strategy day.
There's a lot at stake because southern and western Australia account for nearly 30 percent of Metcash IGA trade moves and generate higher turnover than the Metcash IGA network in eastern states.
Customers in South and Western Australia are notoriously provincial, and IGA stores enjoy a much stronger market share than in NSW, Viktoria and Queensland - more than 30 per cent in SA and 24 per cent in WA compared with around 14 percent in eastern states.
In any case, analysts such as Morgan Stanley believe that Aldi fields 5 percent of the food market in these two states within 12 months of the opening of the first stores, which has received sales from all existing players, but mainly independent retailers.
This week in his report, Morgan Stanley analyst, Tom Kierath, estimated that IGA retail could lose US$360 million on annual sales and Metcash of US$250 million at the end of 2016.
This forecast is based on the assumption that Aldi will open 25 stores in South Australia and 30 stores in the West over the next year, and will generate US$500 and US$600 million sales in individual markets.
The loss of food sales and volumes in Metcash's fixed price base could cost it around US$13 million on pre-tax revenues, Mr Kierath confirmed.
At the same time, Metcash is losing shares in markets in the eastern states, mainly against Aldi and Coles, although Roy Morgan's latest survey found that IGA shoppers were the most loyal.
Morgan Stanley thinks Metcash food sales will fall by 3.8 percent in 2016, 4.1 percent in 2017 and 2.1 percent in 2018, and its market share will decrease from 14 percent in 2015 to 10.3 percent by 2020.
Last week, Hamish Douglass, the founder of the Magellan financial group, said business lunches in Melbourne could "highly disappear within a decade."
Metcash declined to publicly respond to Douglass' comments.
But it's known that Metcash board member contacted Douglass last week.
IGA's market share has fallen from 18 percent to 14 percent since 2010, and earnings from Metcash food business, which supplies about 1200 IGA retail stores , fell 43 percent since 2012, reflecting the operating evolution of the change in speculation as sales and revenues are under pressure.
However, wholesale has gone counterattack and are investing more than US$150 million a year to match counter prices in Coles and Woolworths on hundreds of products each week, helping IGA retailers re-equip stores by adding fast-growing food and novelties from private individuals.
Metcash's chief executive, Ian Morrice, argues that price-comparing programs and retrofitting of stores will take a slope and hope that the wholesale will go back to basic profit growth in 2017 for the first time in four years.
Thanasi Kokkinakis endorsed by Tenning Australia President Steve Healy
Thanasi Kokkinakis deserves fame rather than criticism for his behavior.
Thanasi Kokkinakis has become a side-feasure during a recent fuss around his friend Nick Kyrgios and deserves praise rather than criticism for his behaviour, according to Tenin Australia president Steve Healy.
Kokkinakis, a younger member of a talented duo often called "Special Ks," drew unwelcome attention to himself thanks to an affair with the infamous Kyrgios and Stan Wawrinka at the Montreal Masters.
Soon afterwards, in a passionate qualifying match in Cincinnati, Kokkinakis and his opponent Ryan Harrison had to be separated twice by referees, Harrison then declared: "Wawrinka was supposed to stretch one to Kyrgios, and I was supposed to stretch to him, too."
While he was last seen smashing the racket after a cramp thwarted Richard Gasquet's possible climbing at the U.S. Open, Kokkinakis unwaveringly defended Healy.
The youngster is currently located in Glasgow, where he competes with Sam Groth for a second-place finish behind Bernard Romice in the Davis Cup semi-finals against Britain.
"I've been thinking quite intensely that, in my opinion, Thanasi has been a bit involved in the whole dispute," Healy says.
He ruined his racket when he lost because of a cramp in the U.S., but he's not the first player to take out anger on his racket because of his frustration. I know Thanasi pretty well and I can say he won't be proud of the event.
But he's been in great control very well because he's been subjected to very provocative circumstances in the U.S. because of Harrison and so on, so he deserves great recognition in my view.
He's got a fantastic team around him, he's also got a great family around him and I think he's been treated somewhat hard.
He's a very mature 19-year-old man, he works very hard and fits the team very well and so on, he's never had a problem with the other players, he's very respected in the dressing room, he's trained hard, he's in amazing shape, he's a very stable and mature young kid and I just think he deserves to be judged by his own merits.
Healy also backed the decision to overlook Kyrgios because of an important match that begins on Friday, stating that "mutual decisions" is the right solution for everyone, but he supports the Canberry native to return to the team as a long-term star.
"First of all, we want our team to be completely focused on the game and not on all the controversy that [Kyrgios] has created around him," Healy says.
And I think Nick has risened very fast as Federer, Murray and other people point out, he's growing rapidly in the eyes of the public, he's also made some terrible mistakes and he deserves all the criticism he's getting, but we're trying to support him, we're trying to help him understand the consequences of his actions and for some time outside the limelight when he'll be able to think about it is, in my opinion, the best solution for the team and himself.
Europe's migration crisis: Hungary declares state of emergency, closes borders and detains migrants
After announcing a state of emergency, Hungary closed its southern border with Serbia on Tuesday and detained those who tried to cross it illegally so as to halt the influx of migrants.
There was chaos at the border, while hundreds of migrants accumulated in no-man's country and Serbian authorities reacted outrageously.
Those stranded for an unfamiliar time on the belts of the country between the two states stand those fleeing violence in their native countries, building tents and settling up.
But feelings of frustration come.
When a helicopter flies over them, the migrants collectively call "Open the border!" and scold the riot squad of the Hungarian police.
Some in protest refuse food and water.
Serbia's foreign minister declares that it was "unacceptable" for refugees to be sent back from Hungary, while more and more are arriving from Macedonia and Greece.
(Serbia) wants to be part of the solution, and not just indirect harm.
Over the next few days it will be necessary to discuss with Brussels and other countries," Ivica Dacic said in Prague.
The unrest on the Hungarian-Serbian border came a day after a bloc of 28 states did not come up with a united immigration policy at continued meetings in Brussels.
The ministers agreed to share responsibility for 40 000 people seeking protection in surprised Italy and Greece and spoke with the hope of reaching an eventual agreement for another month or at the end of the year, by which EU nations would accept more than 120 000 additional refugees, including Hungary.
German Federal Chancellor Angela Merkel and Austria convened a special EU European summit on Tuesday for another week to discuss the continental refugee crisis.
Hungary, however, did not fix its hopes on Brussels or its neighbours.
Tuesday's state of emergency in the two southern regions gave the authorities greater power to deal with the crisis and allowed them to close the roads and speed up court cases of asylum hearings.
In the meantime, Hungarian foreign minister Peter Szijjarto said his country is also planning to build a barbed-wire fence along the border with Romania to halt refugees who are trying to cross their country as a fence on the Serbian border is now completed.
CBS News correspondent Charlie ́Agata reported that even prisoners were on the list of those who helped stretch barbed wire between Hungary and Serbia.
In recent months, Hungary has become a major weakness and an entry point into the European Union for refugees, many of whom have fled Syria and other Middle Eastern countries.
However, Prime Minister Viktor Orban insisted that most of them are economic migrants who go for a better life and not war refugees seeking protection, a view that is clearly different from other EU states, including Germany.
The new laws, which came into force at midnight in Hungary, are now criminalizing the violation of the 4-metre fence that the government had built on the southern border with Serbia, and also setting longer periods in prison for those who illegally transfer refugees.
"Thanks to the situation caused by mass migration, the Hungarian government declares a state of threat," government spokesman Zoltan Kovacs told reporters in the southern city of Szeged.
We are quite clear on this: illegal crossing of the border is a crime.
Technically, parliament still has to approve the deployment of the army, which was expected in the next week, but Associated Press reporters at the border have been observing heavily armed troops with vehicles and dogs for several days.
Gyorgy Bakondi, Orbana's adviser for domestic defense, said the authorities caught 45 people who tried to cross the border and caught 15 already on the state's territory.
They have penetrated using fence damage and are now charged in police custody of committing offences under new laws.
Authorities quickly fixed the fence.
Hungarian authorities also closed two out of seven border crossings with Serbia on Tuesday morning after using a barbed-wired tow truck.
There was chaos at Roszke's main border crossing because the Hungarians opened a small office in a metal processing container and there were crowds of people.
About 20 of them fit in, but thousands stayed outside.
Another group of refugees blocked the main highway linking Serbia and Hungary when they refused food and water until they were allowed to cross the border into Hungary.
Election 2016: Hillary Clinton's lead over Bernie Sanders was halved in the national vote
Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton still holds a double-digit lead in the Democratic race for national nominations, but Senator Bernie Sanders is catching up with her lead.
Clinton is now backed by 47 percent of Democratic primary voters (a 58 percent drop), while Sanders is in second place with 27 percent (an increase from 17 percent).
Vice-president Joe Biden, who is yet to announce whether he will want to become president, is gaining support from 15 percent of Democratic primary electorates.
More than five months prior to the start of major competitions, most democratic voters say it's too early to say that they are clearly a decision which candidate they will support.
But Clinton's sponsors are more confident in their choice than those who support other candidates.
Since last month's CBS News poll, Clinton has lost ground in several demographic groups, while Sanders has won several.
Clinton maintains a big lead among women and mitigates, but her leadership narrows.
Her support among men has declined significantly, and Sanders loses only 5 points to her.
Clinton's advantage with Democratic voters under the age of 50 has evaporated, and they are now equating with Sanders.
Clinton, however, is still in great leadership thanks to older voters.
Last month, Clinton had a double-digit lead over Sanders among the Liberals, but now Sanders is losing only 5 points to her in that group.
About half of Democratic primary voters across the states say they would enthusiastically support Clinton if she became the party's nominee.
A quarter would support her with certain reservations, and another 15 percent would support her only because she's a candidate.
Six percent wouldn't support her.
It's probably no surprise that Clinton's current attachments are particularly inflamed because of her.
Views are mixed among those that don't support Clinton at the moment - only about a quarter would enthusiastically support her.
While 48 percent of Democratic primary-electors say they would enthusiastically support Clinton, less Republican voters say the same about their favorite, Donald Trump (35 percent).
When Clinton's supporters are asked in an open question about why they want her as a candidate, the most common answer is that she has the right experience (16 percent), it's time for a woman to be president (13 percent), and that she's the best nominee for the job (10 percent).
Clinton supporters also mention her relationship with Bill Clinton (9 percent).
Furthermore, a great possibility of choice and agreement on problems is cited.
While the vice president is considering a potential candidacy for president, 57 per cent of Democratic primary electorate would like the lead to it - a third in turn think he shouldn't run.
When primary Democratic voters were asked who they would choose as their second choice for the party's nomination, Biden's most common choice.
Among Clinton supporters, more than half listed Biden as their second choice, far ahead of Sanders.
If Biden decides not to run for the Democrats' nomination, Hillary Clinton's leadership will expand from 20 points when Biden run, to 30 points without his candidacy.
Fifty-five percent of Democrat voters see Clinton as a candidate with the best chance of winning the general election; however, that number fell from 78 percent last month.
Biden is far second on this scale, but the percentage of people who see him as most likely chosen is increasing.
However, when it came to the question of the candidate's greater importance, Democratic voters prefer solving problems over a better chance of being elected.
Similar to last month, not even one Democrat candidate or a potential candidate acts as an unacceptable candidate for these primary voters.
Democratic candidates or potential candidates, have good results in some key characteristics among their party's primary voters.
Most see Clinton, Sanders and Biden as sincere and trustworthy and think they have strong leadership qualities.
Biden, out of all three, leads in sincerity, while Clinton is a strong leader.
Thirty percent of Democratic primary voters don't think Clinton is honest and trustworthy, twice as much as those who say the same thing about Sanders (15 percent), but 27 percent don't have that view on him.
Primary Democratic voters also generally see that Clinton, Sanders and Biden are consistent with their ideological outlook.
As for all of these standards, about a quarter of Democratic voters have no opinion of Sanders.
As questions about Hillary Clinton’s decision to use her personal email and the server as secretary of state continue, the majority of primary Democratic voters are satisfied with explaining the matter, saying it didn’t affect their overall view of it at all.
But it's a different story among the American public overall.
Half of Americans are now dissatisfied with her explanation, while only about one-third are satisfied.
The public's views on the subject were split as early as March, shortly after the issue came to light.
Most Americans say this email controversy has no effect on their overall opinion about Clinton, while at 36 percent their opinion of Clinton has deteriorated.
It's mainly about Republicans, where more than 6 out of 10 say their view of Clinton has deteriorated.
Even though Clinton is seen as honorable and trustworthy among her party's constituents, this issue is still a problem among registered voters overall: only 32 percent see her as honorable and trustworthy, a decline of eight points from last month, and by 15 points since May.
Sixty-two percent of registered voters don't think it's honest and credible.
At the same time, 55 percent of registered voters across the country don't see Trump as honest and credible either.
However, both Clinton and Trump have good grades for leadership.
Of the six candidates tested, Biden, Carson and Bush did their best.
Sanders sees more people as honest than dishonest, but, as with Carson, a lot of voters didn't make an opinion about him.
The margin of errors for a sample of 351 primary Democratic voters is 6 percentage points.
Ben Carson finished behind Donald Trump in national elections
Ben Carson is approaching Donald Trump in the latest CBS News/New York Times poll
Two candidates are responsible for half of the electorate in the latest poll, and the others are leaning support.
Republican voters across the country continue to support Trump by an overwhelming majority, and have climbed from 23% support in the latest CBS poll conducted ahead of the Fox News debate last month, to 27% on Tuesday.
But Carson fired from 6 to 23% in the same period.
The poll also found Carson doing well across demographics, decommissioning Trump among university-educated Republican voters.
The remainder of the field, with the exception of Carly Fiorin and Ohio Governor John Kasich, is left behind by Wisconsin Governor Scott Walker, who is completely on track from 10% support, dropped to the current 2%.
Former Florida Governor Jeb Bush, former Arkansas Governor Mike Huckabee and Florida Senator Marco Rubio are all in third-place with 6%.
The margin of errors in a sample of 376 Republican voters in the latest poll is plus or minus 6% and took place in September.
The latest CNN/ORC poll released last week showed Carson had improved, achieving 19% support among Republicans for Trump's 32% support.
The latest poll from the ABC News/Washington Post also showed that Trump is significantly ahead of Carson.
In Carson's rising trend, Trump has decided to shoot a retired neurosurgeon, and has even apologised to Carson for interrogation about Trump's beliefs.
"We need energy," Trump declared last Saturday during the Iowa campaign.
Five fraternity members accused of death of first-year student Baruch College
Nearly two years after a student died during an overeising fraternal ritual in Pocono Mountains, Pensylvania, she was filed a murder charge against five people.
Members of New York's Baruch College Pi Delta Dogs brutally attacked Chun "Michael" Deng during the opening ceremony in September 2013 and delayed took him to hospital, contributing to his death, police announced.
Police say he was blindfolded, wearing a 30-pound backpack full of sand, and was repeatedly harassed while trying to reach his target through a frozen field.
Mr. Deng eventually complained of a headache and then fell unconscious.
Members of the Pi Delta Dog Brotherhood didn't call 911 immediately after Deng fainted and reportedly waited an hour before taking him to hospital, police say.
However, they found time to disguise Deng's dress, google his symptoms and contact Andy Mengo, the Brotherhood's national president, and ask him for advice, Police Chief Chris Vagner said at a news conference.
"At this point, members began to hide equipment and basically preferred the well-being of brotherhood to the life of Michael Deng," Chief Wagner said in a statement.
He failed to revive him and died the next morning.
According to an autopsy report, Deng was repeatedly struck by a blunt object to the head, torso and thighs.
"Too many families have been devastated as a result of overburdening in fraternities, and at least one student dies every year because of overburdening since 1970," Douglas Fierberg, a lawyer representing Deng's family, wrote in a statement.
Brotherhood and their members must take responsibility, and this step is important on the part of the institutions.
The Monroe Countie grand jury has recommended third-degree homicide charges for the top five members.
Thirty-two other members of the fraternity, including Mr. Mengo, will also be charged with assault by overloading and criminal conspiracy.
"We want to detain not only the people involved, but if there is an organization that approved it, whether officially or unofficially, we will hold them accountable, too," Wagner said at Tuesday's news conference.
Baruch College has banned fraternity and national fraternity has dissolved its relationship with local branches.
This with tents when the Fed raises interest rates
Rate growth will come and the bull market will stumble, bond yields will rise, and the economy will slide into recession.
We know all of this.
What we don't know is how much time it's going to take and how long it's going to take
For the economy specifically, the history of the small originator offers about the timing.
The recession came 11 months after the first rate hike and lasted 86 months.
The aggressiveness of the Federal Reservoid in raising rates is often, though not always, determining how the economy and financial assets will react.
That's why U.S. central bank officials have stressed so strongly that investors shouldn't be focused on when rate hikes begin, but rather the trajectory of how long it will take normalisation.
There are actually more variables in the game.
However, in the end, members of the market can make sure that all fuss because of the increase in speed can be exaggerated.
"The Fed's first increase since the global financial crisis will inevitably be interpreted by some as the end of the era of 'cheap money,'" Julian Jessop, a senior global economist at Capital Economics, said in a note.
By contrast, we do not expect the gradual return of U.S. interest rates to more normal, but still low, will create a seismic shock that many might be afraid of.
This means that there will be adverse effects.
Here's a look at how certain areas of the economy will respond, based on historical trends:
Due to what the market has seen in about a month, the expected increase in tariffs can make the air a little unstable.
However, once the increase occurs, the effect will not be so dramatic.
"There appears to be a trend for re-cessing stocks after 12-24 months after the first increase, which again may reflect a delay in monetary policy," Deutsche Bank analysts said in a recent study of what will happen after the Fed's increase.
More specifically, the market over the past roughly 35 years is often heading for a rate hike in relatively flat 250 days (an average increase of 2.6 percent), and then returns to normal again after 500 days, with an average yield of the last six cycles of 14.4 percent, according to a recent analysis by Bob Doll, chief equity strategist at Nuveen Asset Management, posted on Barron.
Deutsche said the impact on stocks tended to become more pronounced later in the cycle of rate hikes and the return is beginning to decrease.
A recession is a fact of economic life, but rate hikes often help it.
At present, the Fed is facing some conditions that have not yet existed and that could hasten the recession.
Most notable is that the gross domestic product near its lowest point in history when the Fed's rates rise.
According to Deutsche Bank, during an 118-rate increase since 1950, GDP was only twice the nominal year-on-year increase below 4.5 percent.
Even though it was 3.6 percent in the second quarter of 2015, few expected it to be sustained when it was at just 1.5 percent in the third quarter, according to the Atlanta Fed.
Raising rates in this complex economic background could be risky and a question could emerge "whether this time is different," Deutsche said.
Market participants were ready to grow the Fed all year long, and all eyes turned their eyes to the meeting of the Federal Free Market Committee, which took place this week and ends on Thursday.
While many strategists and economists believe FOMC could approve a rise at that meeting, future trading indicates only a 25 percent probability.
"In our study since 1950, all increasing cycles have so far been in a super-cycle of leverage increases with GDP obscuring the peaks at the beginning of the recession very quickly after an incipient recovery," the report said.
On the contrary, this was clearly a slow recovery since the worst recession in the sampling period.
The current cycle is by far the longest of those that the Fed has been waiting since the end of the last recession; the record was 35 months, we are now at 74 months and still going on.
The firm return has also been volatile because the market expects a rate hike and the pattern is somewhat similar to what stocks are experiencing.
The main difference is that the impact is more quickly evident in bonds than in stocks when the Fed changes policy direction.
For bonds, yields seem to change direction immediately after the first arrival of the increase/decrease cycle.
At the end of the cycle, bond yields are falling immediately," Deutsche said.
Charles Schwab's strategists believe the rate hike will cause yields approaching long- and short-dated bonds, equating the curve.
High-yield bonds often work better in this climate, although "we're still cautious about stretching the return," Kathy Jones, a fixed-retirement analyst at Schwab, said in her analysis.
Interestingly, Jones thinks the markets are already adapted to expectations of the Fed's policies.
"In addition to the strength of the dollar and the decline in inflation expectations, there are several signs that the market has adapted to the prospects of tighter monetary policy," she wrote.
Short-term interest rates are above, the yield curve is flatter, the credit margins have widened, and volatility has increased - all market characteristics when the Fed tightens policy.
Generally speaking, firms that carry out most of their business in the U.S
Multinational companies with lots of debt will do worse, because the rising dollar will make their products more expensive in the space of the world market, and their debt will also be greater.
"History shows that the "quality" of stocks tends to overcome over the course of three months after the initial rate hike," Goldman Sachsve analysts said in a report for clients.
Firms with strong balance sheets overtook weak balance sheets in every interest rate hike in 1994, 1999, 2004 by an average of 5 percentage points.
Companies with high returns on capital, as well as low-volatility stocks, have also outperformed their lower-quality counterparts by an average of 4 (percentage points), respectively 3 (percentage points).
Debt will become a big problem.
Companies with a high percentage of floating interest rates will lose the most, Goldman said.
In addition to pure stock games, consumers are also benefiting because of the rising dollar.
Saviers can see their profits also through higher returns, but experts have different opinions about how long it will last.
