Internet sales are growing at the slowest pace since August and Black Friday may be to blame
The first three months of 2015 saw a 7% increase in online sales
Last month saw the lowest growth since the records themselves began in 2000
Online sales were boosted three years ago thanks to mobile shopping
Experts believe that November's Black Friday could be the reason for the braking of spending
More than a decade, virtual shopping has risen, and thanks to it, many of Britain's main streets have become ghost towns.
However, experts say there are signs of settling online sales, and new data show a seven percent increase in the first three months of 2015.
Overall spending for August represented the lowest increase in growth since the very beginning of the 2000 measurement, with an increase of just 5 percent compared to last year.
Experts say there are signs of settling online sales, and new data shows a seven percent increase in the first three months of 2015.
“When you look at the level of growth over the past years and you go back to 2000, the situation has always been rationalized over time,” Andy Mulcahy, editor of the IMRG revenue index, told the Guardian.
