Experts criticise a review of electronics published by Public Head England
A statement by a government-funded agency that e-cigarettes are 95% safer than smoking came from a meeting of 12 people, some of whom are linked to the tobacco industry, the researchers say.
Experts at the British Medical Journal (BMJ) have joined Lancet's criticism of the evidence used in its e-cigarette report by Public Health England (PHE).
PHE published a "minical" report last month describing it as a "comprehensive review of evidence."
However, several researchers have questioned the data's comprehension and highlighted links between some experts and the tobacco industry.
An editorial in the professional journal Lancet last month attacked an "extraordinarily flimsy base" on which the PHE agency based its main conclusions.
Two other experts writing for the BMJ have questioned claims that were allegedly "built on rock or sand."
Martin McKee, professor of European public health at the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, and Simon Capewell, a professor of clinical epidemiology at the University of Liverpool, said: "The fundamental principle of public health is that politics should be based on evidence of effectiveness."
They indicate that the public could expect that PHE's assertion, which reads "the current best estimation is that e-cigarettes are around 95% less harmful than smoking" are based on a detailed review of evidence and modelling.
In fact, this conclusion comes from one meeting of 12 people convened to prepare a multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) model to synthesize their views on the damage associated with different nicotine-containing products; the results of the negotiations were summarized in the research report.
McKee and Capewell report that one sponsor of the meeting was a company called EuroSwiss Health, whose CEO has reportedly previously received funding from British American Tobacco for independent studies.
He has also endorsed BAT's public health credentials in a sustainability report, he states.
One of the 12 participants in the meeting acknowledged funding from an e-cigarette maker, but not the funding it allegedly received previously from tobacco company Philip Morris International, they add.
There is no reason to select panel members, but they contain several well-known e-cigarette champions, some of whom also declare industrial funding in the paper.
Some others present at the meeting are unknown for their expertise in the fight against smoking.
The meeting in PHE was also attended by tobacco management.
A report aroused in this group "eloquently concedes," missing "hard evidence of the harmfulness of most products on most criteria," McKee and Capewell wrote.
However, none of these links or restrictions are described in the PHE report.
McKee and Capewell said PHE's claims that "there is no evidence yet that e-cigarettes act as a route to smoking for children or non-smokers," was premature.
Professor Kevin Fenton, director of health and well-being at PHE, said the claims at the BMJ had already been answered.
“Electronic cigarettes are significantly less harmful than smoking.”
One of two lifelong smokers dies because of their addiction.
All the evidence suggests that the health risks posed by e-cigarettes are small in comparison, but we need to continue to examine the long-term effects.
PHE has a clear obligation to inform the public about what the evidence shows, and also what it doesn't display, especially when there has been so much public confusion about the relative dangers compared with tobacco.
Nearly 80,000 people die from smoking-related illnesses every year, and smoking costs NHS ₣££££££2 a year.
By clearly highlighting the current evidence - that even if e-cigarettes aren't risk-free, they carry only a fraction of the damage caused by smoking - we're fulfilling our national competence.
