Of course, we all think that 150 British pounds a year is a big sum, but for people for whom there are 150 pounds a lot, I'd be surprised if they paid so much, because they don't look so much at the BBC.
Former Sky and Channel 5 director, David Elstein, who spoke before the commission on Tuesday, said the BBC should revise its Reith mission in the sense of "informing, educating and entertaining."
He confirmed that a small volume of BBC production has now previously been classified as educational - it used to be 1% television production and 0.1% on the radio - and should take public public broadcasting in Australia and Canada as a role model and turn into "inform, clarify and entertain."
On the subject of the partnership, Elstein said that the BBC should do more in areas such as Proms, of which only a handful have been broadcast on television, and connect with commercial reporters so that we can see them all online.
“Sonogenetics” or control of brain cells by means of sound waves
This video shows how sound impulses affect the direction in which nematodes move.
The researchers raised worms with a genetically modified nervous system that can control sound waves.
Small nematodes change direction when they are affected by sound impulses, which are inaudible to the human ear.
Pulses work by switching cells of genetically modified motor neurons that are programmed to respond to these ultrasound waves.
Related: Scientists can read and record brain activity using light
The researchers said these worms are great examples of a new methodology called sonogenetics, which uses ultrasound to activate brain, heart and muscle cells and activates themselves outside the body.
Sreekanth Chalasani, a researcher at the Salk Institute in La Jolla, California, told the Guardian that this method could one day replace deep brain stimulation - an invasive procedure in which electrodes that stimulate the brain are injected into the patient's brain. The method is used, for example, to treat the symptoms of Parkinson's disease.
Nematodes usually do not respond to ultrasound, but Chalasani found that the reaction occurs when placed in a fluid containing microscopic bubbles.
As he discovered, bubbles amplify ultrasound waves, which then pass through the worm body.
Reinforced ultrasonic waves act in TRP-4 ionic channels, which are found in the membranes of some worm cells.
Thanks to the sound waves, these channels open and activate the cells with which they are connected, according to a report by the journal Nature Communications.
In order to control nematodes using ultrasound waves, Chalasani genetically modified the worms so that some of their motoric neurons contain TRP-4 ionic channels.
When he then applied ultrasound to such modified worms, the sound waves were amplified by microbubbles and transferred to the worms, where modified motor neurons were switched on.
The new method in many ways resembles opthogenetics, a pioneering tool that allows scientists to turn on and off neurons using light.
Chalasani, however, was told that sonogenetics could have many advantages over this technique.
Unlike light, which must be sent by optical fiber to the desired location in the brain, ultrasound waves can penetrate the tissue without restrictions and be sent directly to the brain.
"We believe that when we use gene therapy and therapeutic viruses, we can target human neurons and make them temporarily sensitive to ultrasound signals used in clinical treating to treat neurological diseases," Chalasani said.
He added that the method would also find its application in the treatment of insulin-producing muscle cells and cells.
