Eight golden rules for living in a student dormitory
11 years ago, I moved into student dormitories like an oblivious nineteen-year-old boy, still believing in berets and moral relativism, but it's only now that I recall the tense smiles of those I've bothered - and numerous, tedious complaints about others.
Everything I've learned can be distilled into eight simple rules.
Moving to the tracks can range from slightly to the nerves walking to the totally appalling.
It's tempting to babble: tell people where you're from, if you've taken a break for a year, what do you think about Corbyn and why "you honestly don't even hate Marmite, because people, it's just HAHAHA yeast extract, I'll be in my room."
Take a deep breath.
And help others to take a breath too - ask and show interest.
It manages the conversation and helps you find the people you like, and those you wouldn't have fun with even if your eyelashes were on fire and they'd make fire extinguishers.
Leave people space.
People's rooms often turn into drinking receptions, movie evenings and communal public places, but they're also rooms where people dress up and adjust their underwear.
Related: Don't even think about going to college and being in a relationship
Introverts in college will come in short.
Your room should be a refuge, yet a lot of dormitories are transformed into an out-of-the-mood walking culture that is friendly, social and contributes to lifelong friendships.
It can be hell for someone who needs to recharge.
No culture lets you say, "I love you and I want our friendship to continue, but I need you to leave so I can get back to the energy I've spent talking to you."
Try to know when a classmate is tired of socialization - and maybe write to him instead of knocking.
Speaking about knocking: knock.
One of the boys in my dormitories had the habit that if the door wasn't locked, he'd be invited inside.
He's seen a few things.
Make friends through baking.
Because you've started going to a university at the top of baking mania, knocking on someone else's door, and a performance with a slice-baked cookie seems slightly less odd, less than "Welcome to the neighborhood, I've baked something for you, now smile while I ask personal questions."
Providing homemade treats is a good way to make friends.
Your roommates will feel obliged to talk to you, and maybe everything will end up by assigning your face to a tasty surprise.
This, of course, is the object that you're a decent baker.
Poorly condensed pieces of flour, sugar and stiffness are likely to be interrupted by others pretending to sleep if you try to knock on their doors.
Stealing food is a common crime in student dormitories.
Stealing a whole chicken is, of course, a crime, but is a drop of milk into your tea theft?
Check it out twice with the owner of the milk.
There's a chance they'll offer you milk even if they're furious inside.
If someone steals something from you, it's annoying, and you've got my sympathy - but try to deal with it a little more grown-up than my roommates, who exchanged their milk for dishwashing detergent to punish the thieves.
He's in prison now.
You shouldn’t make noises that distract people from their sleep or studies.
It's not just about being a decent human being, it's also about the famous people in glass houses.
The girl from my hallway asked her neighbors if they could quiet the drum and bass because they were 3 at night and she had a test in the morning.
Their response was to amplify music.
When they finally stopped at 5 o'clock in the morning, she let them sleep peacefully for two hours, and then released their own music aloud, locked the door, and left for their three-hour test.
Wouldn't it be romantic if your future would go to the same dormitories as you?
Conversely, wouldn't it be appallingly embarrassing if you mistook your roommates for your future, slept together, broke up, and had to see each other every day, and maybe take control of your mutual friends, too?
Of course, you don't command your heart - but if your sexy roommate isn't your future, try talking to your weaknesses at least a little.
In an uplifting moment when you realize that someone has left the keys in their unlocked room, there is only a thin line between fun and bullying.
A good prank is funny, but it's easy to fix.
Teach them a valuable and entertaining lesson about their security by hiding something from them - never destroy someone's room with toothpaste and shampoo.
Lock their doors and put their keys in a water bottle, which you then put in the freezer - but never stick their belongings on the ceiling more than twice.
Eva was looking forward to the twins despite uncertain prospects for the future.
Eva had a hard time already during her pregnancy with the seven-year-old Vítek.
His father broke up with her, and she was alone to prepare and raise the offspring.
I coped with the situation quite quickly - I was very much looking forward to the child and I was looking for positives.
Uncertainty, and by being alone with my child, I tried not to worry.
And I certainly had the hope that we wouldn't be alone for a long time, Eva today remembers her first pregnancy.
Even then she met on the Internet on the chart of the older Vašek, with whom they began to talk about favorite movies.
Vasek even brought Eva and Vítek home from the maternity hospital, but he didn't rush into the role of stepdad.
"He then withdrew from the program for a while," Eva described in the documentary series Four in That 3.
The motif of the third season of the Czech television series is the search for happiness for the second attempt.
Thanks to her part-time work and the great help of her mom and her aunt’s help, she managed to take care of Vítek and her family’s financial security.
Even the prospects of responsibility for a foreign child did not spoil Vašek’s affection for Eve.
They definitely got together around Vítek's second birthday, and he now understands Vaška as his dad and addresses him that way.
Even though Vašek already has two adult daughters, he enjoys fatherhood at sixty, according to his words, and devotes more attention to children.
In addition to Vítek, two-year-old Leontýnka was added to them, and finally twins.
"Vašek came into our family somehow naturally, maybe even because my mother had never spoken much into my relationships," Eva thinks.
Not enough space?
At least we're close.
Twins Emma and Ela were born by caesarean section after Eva was diagnosed with pregnancy cholestasis at the end of her pregnancy.
This is a malfunction in the production and activity of bile acids, which are then found in an excessive amount in the pregnant blood and are manifested mainly by itching.
The disease is dangerous for the unborned fetus, which is at risk of premature birth and death, in the mother after childbirth it usually subsists spontaneously.
Ema and Ela were born healthy for Eva and Vaška's happiness, even though they were only at 36 weeks.
They placed Emička in an incubator in the intermediary department, where Vaška's niece also works, who managed to take over and accommodate her.
Elinka was bigger and immediately after transferring from ICU to my room I had it with me, describes a challenging period after childbirth Eva.
She spent twenty days in maternitary, until the doctors agreed to release the babies, which, meanwhile, managed to reach the two-pound limit.
Waiting to collect children in the maternity hospital did not make Eva happy.
"I've been crazy about my husband and children," the four-time mother recalls of the difficult moments in the six-month-old ward.
She and her husband were also considering signing a reverse, among other things due to the insensitive attitude of one of the doctors.
However, this did not happen in the end, and the doctors discharged Eva and the twins into home care.
Stories of four pregnancies and childbirths are broadcast by ČT1 at 21:45 on Tuesday evening.
Quite a large family is able to humble themselves.
They live together in a small house near Prague, where Eva had to make do with an electric two-burner and cramped conditions.
The house did not increase, but Vašek made Eva happy by installing the gas cooker to make the preparation of food for six people more comfortable.
If he's at his best, we'll take turns taking turns for a taxi.
Eva is convinced that the children perceive, in particular, family well-being and a pleasant atmosphere, and not the size or oscality of the house.
For now, we've assembled very nicely, better than we expected.
The mess is harder to hide in a small space, and we sometimes cross round babies, but at least we are all close to each other, Eva smiles, who manages to find a positive side on every situation.
Vaška's work of the illuminator in the film is not a guarantee of regular reception.
That's why he sometimes takes to the streets of Prague as a taxi driver.
At the age of sixty, according to him, he physically manages to care for the finances of families and children, but how long will it be?
"I realize that, due to our age difference, it will be me who will feed this family," Eva admits flatly when asked about the future.
She'd like to go back to work, but she's even more attracted to the possibility of her own business.
With four kids, I'm not exactly a terro for employers.
And if it's at our worst, my husband and I can take turns taking a taxi, a sympathetic blonde jokes.
Apple suffered a yo-yo effect.
It's all because of displays.
A more powerful processor, a higher-resolution camera, a tighter chassis or a touch-recognizating display, are in short the advantages of the recently unveiled ninth-generation iPhones.
The last two also have a negative impact on the dimensions and weights of iPhones 6s and 6s Plus.
Apple’s trend so far has been that every new generation of the iPhone is lighter than the previous one.
The exceptions were only the third and fourth generations, whose weight increased slightly compared to previous iPhones.
The two-gram weight difference to the detriment of the novelty was really negligible.
However, this is far from the case with new iPhones.
Compared to last year's iPhones 6 and 6 Plus, the weight increased by 14 grams for a smaller model, and by 20 grams for the larger one.
On the one hand, it's not surprising, last year's bending affair in Apple's pockets suffered greatly.
The chassis of the novelties is made of the more durable aluminum alloy 7000, which was also reflected in the weight.
But displays have a much greater impact on it.
Apple has used 3D Touch technology for new features, thanks to which displays recognize the power of touch.
To do this, it was necessary to provide the display with an additional layer of capacitive sensors.
Thus, the weight of the displays of the new iPhones has increased more than twice that of previous generations.
For a smaller iPhone from originally 12 grams to 29 grams, in the case of a larger one from 19 to 40 grams.
On the iPhone 6s, the display is the heaviest component used.
The weight of only 143 grams is then achieved with this novelty also thanks to the slimming bark, which touched most of the remaining components.
For a larger model, it is the heaviest element of the battery used, but its weight has not changed compared to its predecessor.
Even in this case, the biggest increase is noticeable with the display.
Thus, the weight of the 5.5-inch iPhone 6s Plus reaches 192 grams.
To the extent that the new display technology is practical, it will be reflected only when used.
The first users will be able to try it on Friday, September 25, when the sale of iPhones 6s and 6s Plus will be launched in selected countries.
Have the flakes of breakfast champions been for tens of thousands of years?
In Grotta Paglicci, Italy, archaeologists managed to find which opens up interesting possibilities.
They discovered traces of modern man from different times, including murals, tool remnants, and so on and so on.
In layer 23A, which according to the tools dates back to about 30 thousand years ago, but scientists have discovered something that is very reminiscent of the remnants of the breakfast buffet.
There were traces of oatmeal on the site, which apparently went through the process of producing today's popular flakes.
During it, the grains are soaked first today, then dried, removed from the packaging, the so-called pluch, then steamed and pressed flat.
In the Paglice cave, it was possible to discover grains that were apparently heated or steamed, and then apparently crushed between the two mill stones - at least this is evidenced by traces of use on two stones in a cave.
Thus, in their publication for the journal PNAS, scientists were unable to prove that there would be a complete process of flaking production in the cave, but the similarity is indeed very striking.
We also have no evidence of how people could eat flakes, but the assumption that it was with some liquid and quite possibly warm is, of course, quite logical.
At the same time, it is a very interesting proof of how intensely people used cereals long before the emergence of agriculture.
If the flake interpretation is correct, the oldest flakes were ahead of agriculture by about twenty thousand years.
