The symbol of the village has come alive.
The wheel of the windmill above Chlum revolves again after years.
It can be seen by far, because it is located on a fifteen-meter-high mast.
People from a wide area call him "the fan on Chlum."
For the municipality with 150 inhabitants became even one of the elements of the municipal emblem, which the municipality gained on the occasion of the meeting of natives in 2010.
"At that time, we applied for a sign, and of course we wanted to have a fan on the emblem, everyone knows it in the vicinity," mayor Jiří Mašek said.
Chlum wind pump is a local technical monument.
It is also a memento of the present: it reminds us of a time when people here fought for high-quality drinking water.
It has been rarity in Chlum in past centuries.
It wasn't until 1910 that Chlum councillors decided on a solution, which then suited until the mid-1980s.
In the village at the turn-off to Číchov, they had a seven-metre-deep well excavated.
Three years later, the well was complemented by a metal structure with a wind pump, which pumped water into the reservoir at the highest point of the village, near the local church.
"The construction was carried out by Antonín Kunc from Hranice in Moravia with a cost of 2 400 crowns," states the commemorative inscription on the ground-flooring fence of the mast structure.
After several repairs and falls of the wind bike, the pummber retired in 1971.
On May 7, 1973, a new chapter of the fight for water began: a new water supply began serving in Chlum that day.
The wind pump remained only a malfunctioning municipal symbol and local rarity.
Dominance was launched at this year's reunions.
It was not untiled in 2008 that Chlumský decided to repair the wind wheel again and bring it into operation again.
They had a replica of the original wheel made - reduced by an average of half a meter.
The bike was mounted on the mast, but in the same year it had to be brought down.
The manufacturer of the replica did not choose the appropriate material and the wheel could not defy gusts of wind.
Then, for several years, Chlum’s landmark rested underived under the roof.
Since June this year, however, the round on the high-mast is spinning again.
On the occasion of the reunion of the natives, the village had a replica of the bike modified and again planted it at the top of the iron tower.
But the wheel draws utility water from the well only to the ambush blind shoulder.
And during the summer we completely disconnected it, because there was not enough water in the well.
At least it turned empty.
"In the autumn, we'll unplug the shaft of the wind wheel from the pump in the suction basket again," Mayor Mašek explained.
Chlum is not the only municipality in the Czech Republic, where the wind wheel reached the municipal emblem, but it is the only village of this kind in the whole Highlands.
They have a similar element in the emblem in honor of local technical monuments, for example, in Chvalkovice and Zelená Hora in the Vyškov region, in Kuželov in the Hodonín region, Partutovice in the Přerov region or in the Vsetín Police.
