No one has explained to me how I should keep accepted refugees in the country if they do not want to live in the Czech Republic.
After talks with his European colleagues, Interior Minister Milan Chovanec (ČSSD) complains that other states are pushing the Czech Republic to accept quotas for the redistribution of refugees, but refusing any discussion.
He also notes that so far no one has explained to him how he should keep the allocated refugees in the Czech Republic if they do not want to live in our country anyway.
"We didn't get the answer, and that's why we wanted the quotas to be deleted from the final text," Chovanec told reporters.
Can you describe more specifically the position with which you came at the meeting of the European Union's interior and foreign ministers?
We don't agree with mandatory quotas as a system that simply can't work.
We can't refer to migrants as bags of salt or flour.
They're living beings who have visions, they're going somewhere, they've got relatives.
We wanted to hear from our colleagues how we would be able to sustain migrants in the territory of the Member States if they were redistributed.
We can't imagine.
We didn't get the answer, and that's why we wanted the quotas to be deleted from the final text.
With other conclusions - functional hotspots, i.e. registration points, border protections, camps outside Europe, a trust fund for African countries and other things, we have no problem with this.
We've been talking about that for a year now and we're saying this is the way.
But saying quotas will solve everything?
Not long ago, we've dealt with forty thousand, now it's a hundred and twenty thousand.
When will it be five hundred thousand?
And how are we going to keep these people in that territory?
Ninety-eight percent of all the people who cross us want to go to Germany.
There's almost no one who wants us, we just don't know how to keep them here.
Other foreigners, Ukrainians, Vietnamese and others who take the Czech Republic as a destination country, receive asylum in our country, but the interest of people from this migration wave, i.e. from Syria and other countries, is very close to zero.
You mentioned that refugees don't really want us, but we've already pledged to accept fifteen hundred people.
Wouldn't it be possible to accept more of them in this way?
We are committed to accepting 1500 people, but with a condition.
We used to say: functional hotspots, border control, military operation.
Even with those 1500, the point is that 1100 people will come with whom we will deal with the asylum procedure.
They'll come to our detention facility and we'll treat them in the same way we'll treat those we've captured.
It is a big question, and we will discuss this with Europe, whether we can detain the people who will be registered in Italy and Greece, possibly in Hungary.
Because Europe treats this in such a way that if they have registration, they cannot be detained.
That is, they will come to our territory with registration, and if there is an agreement that is not yet, it may happen that we will not keep even those 1100 on the territory of the Czech Republic and they will go back to Germany.
If we are able to apprehend them for the duration of the asylum procedure, we will arrange an asylum procedure.
If it is the citizens of Syria, Eritrea, almost 98 percent, according to European statistics, gain international protection.
Then they will be given documents that will allow them to travel around Europe.
And I think that these people will again end up in target countries.
Then there are four hundred people who will be resettled.
That's the second project that's going on so far, and we're only progressing there by debating with the churches about the possibility that they could help them find jobs, accommodation and so on.
But it's a run for a very long haul.
You said that at the meeting of the ministers with us the other states were not willing to discuss.
But isn’t it that the Czech Republic only rejects quotas, but doesn’t it offer any solidarity step to unblock the discussion?
And what exactly do you think it should be a solidarity step?
If the Federal Republic of Germany announces an incentive that all migrants from Syria can reach them, then the message spreads in hours throughout all the camps in Europe, but also in Turkey, Jordan.
It encourages another migration wave that goes to Germany, because they just don't want to join us.
We think that the way is to protect the external borders - simply to deter those migration waves, to help the needy, and to separate economic migrants.
Clearly let them know that they don't have a chance.
Fifty thousand Kosovans came to Germany last year alone.
From safe Kosovo, where we pay all the saturation of this state, including the police, the courts, the prosecutor's offices.
If we managed to separate them and not accept them or accept them only according to the needs of the economies, those fifty percent of what the social systems spend on them in vain today could go to refugees.
Compared to the first day when Germany introduced controls, the number of detainees in the Czech Republic jumped.
Now it's eighty, the first day it was twelve.
How far is it supposed to go, so that border controls are tightened?
We were afraid of capture in the order of hundreds of people.
These are the numbers we're handling.
We still have several hundred vacancies in brick-and-mortar detention facilities.
Of course, all the people we detain will be returned to Austria within seven days.
We're doing it by default.
Austrians are the only ones taking these people away from us, it's complicated with Hungary and the Greeks - that's a completely useless story.
So where's the tipping point?
Is it going to be a thousand a day?
I think it'd have to be several hundred a day.
Two hundred, three hundred people a day two, three consecutive days exhaust our capacities so much that crisis plans will start.
Then I agree with the Prime Minister that he would immediately convene an extraordinary government.
He is very interested in the whole thing, we are in phone contact several times a day.
If the situation arises, the government can sit de facto immediately, and with one resolution it would decide to strengthen the police about the army, let's call it the customs administration, or the prison service.
The extreme option is a state of emergency, which has now been declared by Hungary in some places?
I don't think we can get this far.
Remember that our position is different, we are a national state within Europe, we have the Schengen border only and only at the airport.
I don't know what would have to happen for this to happen on the territory of the Czech Republic, I don't want to spread alarm information.
All the information we have, whether from Moldova, Serbia, Hungary, suggests that Hungarians will try to divert the migration flow to Croatia and Slovenia, which would then mean a direct trip to Austria.
It would affect the Czech Republic very marginally, almost at all.
Slavia repaid a large part of the debt at the last minute.
They are trying to prevent bankruptcy.
Soccer Slavia paid debts.
Football players also raised their money.
Football Slavia stood trial on Tuesday for the first time because of insolvency proceedings.
Nearly three months after, because of about 20 million crowns, Online Services from the Pacific Samoa filed for it.
Slavia’s lawyer, Zuzana Mládková, tried to convince Judge Michaela Wenzlová within a few hours that she wasn’t supposed to declare bankruptcy over Slavia.
It must be said that the argument was.
One of the main arguments was that last week, a large Chinese industrial and investment group CEFC and Czech billionaire Jiří Šimáně became the new owner of Slavia.
Clearly from the new owners, money has arrived at the club, at the last minute.
This is also evidenced by Slavia's statement before Tuesday's court hearing - the Vršovice club on Monday, just a day before the hearing in court, sent tens of millions of crowns owed.
"Everything was reimbursed between September 11 and September 15," attorney Slavie Mládková said.
To whom did Slavia pay?
Current and former players and business partners.
Specifically, for example, Slovak ex-representative Milan Ivan or Czech midfielder Mario Lička.
Slavia also paid a fee to agent Alexander Losenick.
Already on Friday, the club also paid the owed fees of the Prague 10 district.
The insolvency dispute thus de facto remains only the claims that Slavie contradicts.
Either according to the club, the given obligations did not arise at all, or litigation was filed.
However, in the event of losing these disputes, we are ready to pay the amounts.
"We have the necessary funds in our bank account, which we can prove with an account statement," attorney Mládková told the judge.
"Everything that has been overdue is reimbursed," Mládková said, adding that only outstanding or discrepanted liabilities were outstanding.
It is also certain that in the next few days the judge will not decide and the proceedings will continue.
She gave Slavia three weeks to send detailed evidence that she had actually paid those debts.
Subsequently, all the evidence will be evaluated.
The decision can therefore be expected in November at the earliest.
The judge ordered the continuation of the hearing on the third of November.
However, the decision will also not be made at this meeting, but only in writing.
