Blanka could be built in such a way that he did not fail.
But it would cost half a billion more, says an expert from the firm that designed the tunnel.
Blanka could have been minted more safely, says an expert.
It was possible to build the Blanka Tunnel Complex in such a way that there would not be two slumps of soil in Stromovka in Prague, according to the production director of Satra, which is the main designer of the tunnel, Alexandr Butovič.
He said this on Wednesday evening during a journalistic tour of the tunnel.
According to him, it would be about half a billion crowns more expensive.
It will open to drivers on Saturday afternoon.
It's always a compromise between price and security.
"The tunnel could have been minted more securely, but the costs are rising exponentially," Butovich said.
According to him, the conditions in Stromovka were the same all the time, yet the country fell just a few steps from the place from which it began to minute with a safer and more cautious method.
"If those measures had been applied 500 metres earlier, the tunnel would have been half a billion more expensive," Butovich said.
Blanka tunnel collapsed three times during construction.
Twice, in 2008, a crater was established in Stromovka, in 2010 the soil collapsed at the Ministry of Culture in Prague 6.
According to Metrostav CEO Jiří Bělohlav, the tunnel is unnecessarily expensive, he told the Euro weekly.
According to the original intention, the construction was supposed to cost 17 billion crowns.
If we add to this the VAT, which has jumped twice during the construction period, out of seventeen billion you have twenty-one.
Adding to this technology, land purchases and engineering activities, valuations and similar items, it's 29 billion, which is no longer that far away from those 37, Bělohlav said
