iOS 9: How to get a new iPhone operating system with a new Apple update
The new operating system can be requested in Settings when you click on the "General" tab and choose "Software Update".
When it’s ready, your phone offers you the option to download and then install it.
Some users faced the problem after installation, specifically with the error message “the software update failed”.
One of the solutions to this problem is to wait until the interest in the update disappears, or to try for it through iTunes.
The phone will guide you through the update process.
This includes the need to uninstall apps to free up space - however, this won't be necessary, as Apple downgraded the update and also facilitated the process by using a special tool to design apps to delete and re-install after the update.
Make sure that your phone is charged before you start the update.
If the battery status is low, Apple won't allow the update to run, because interrupting the process could cause an update error.
Other news: the release of iOS 9 has not been delayed, despite WatchOS delays; a review of iOS 9: an exciting update to the new version of the operating system; iOS9: how to prepare for the release of a new operating system for iPhone
Make sure you’ve gone through our list of things to do before updating which to try and prevent or protect yourself from. iOS 9 will introduce new features, including an even smarter personal assistant and a wide range of creative features for the iPad, as well as many patches to speed up and repair your operating system.
FedEx's profits mixed, reduction of fiscal outlook for 2016
FedEx reported unsatisfactory results for the last quarter and a huge reduction in full-year profit forecasts due to low demand for freight and higher costs for the land division.
The company also said it plans to hire about 55,000 seasonal workers over the holidays.
In this period last year, FedEx announced that it would hire 50,000 seasonal workers for the holidays.
The company's shares fell about 2.5 percent in pre-market trading 45 minutes before Wednesday's opening.
FedEx says it expects earnings of between $10.40 and $10.90 for the fiscal year ending next May, a decline of 20 cents from the previous forecast.
Analysts expected £110.84, according to a survey conducted by FactSet.
The reduced outlook comes despite an interest rate hike of an average of 4.9 per cent, which will become apparent in January. 4, and higher surcharges for the land transport of heavy or large packages, which begin in November.
The company said it was still expecting earnings growth compared to the previous year due to cost reductions, higher revenues and growth in online trading.
The company's chairman and chief executive, Fred Smith, said the company was "showing solid performance despite weaker-than-expected economic conditions, particularly in manufacturing and global trade."
Honeymoning plans were announced a day after rival company UPS said it had plans to hire 90,000 up to 95,000 extra workers, which is about the same as they hired last year.
In a quarter that ended in August. 31, FedEx earned €$692 million, up 6 percent from last year.
Profit per share was $2.42, less than $2.44 per share, which was predicted by 12 analysts surveyed by Zacks Investment Research.
Revenue rose 5 percent, to $12.3 billion.
Six analysts polled by Zacks expected £$12.23 billion.
FedEx has been hit by a decline in priority international shipping in recent years, causing revenues in its Express unit to stagnate.
But the boom in online shopping has boosted the results of FedEx's
In the last quarter, ground revenue rose 29 percent, but operating profit was essentially the same because of higher prices for larger packages and own insurance.
The express unit recorded higher incomes due to higher base rates, while profits fell in freight trade due to higher labour prices.
In pre-market trading about an hour before opening, FedEx shares fell by £3.85, or by 2.5 percent, to $150.15.
They ended Tuesday with a drop of 11 percent for the year and 17 percent below the June 11 record.
In its most anticipated session for years, federal reserve lawmakers will spend two days this week seated at the 27-hundred-length Honduran Mahogany table, deciding whether, after nearly a decade, the time has come to raise the key interest rate.
Towering above them at one end of the central bank's ornament, a two-storey meeting room will be a mural of the United States.
The map is a reminder that Fed Chairman Janet L. Yellen and her colleagues are supposed to put the nation's economic interests above everything else.
The central bank has a double charge from Congress: maximizing U.S. employment and keeping prices stable here.
But since the Great Recession, Fed officials have acknowledged that there is an unwritten third term: financial stability.
And that third term could be an X-factor too close to the foreseeable decision to be announced on Thursday about whether the U.S. economy is ready to be triggered when weaned from an unprecedented stimulus in the form of near-zero short-term interest rates in place since the end of 2008.
Although most of the data shows solid economic growth, recent turbulences in the world's financial markets could scare the already cautious Fed because of the addition to instability by raising its federal fund reference rate - even no more than by just a quarter of a percentage point.
"Clearly, the labor market and inflation will be the most important thing to watch in the first place," said Lindsey M. Piegza, chief economist at brokerage firm Stifel Nicolaus & Co.
"But I think they were just looking for an excuse not to raise the rate, the increased uncertainty surrounding the global market is already giving them sufficient justification to wait further," she said.
There is no consensus about what the Fed will do, which in itself is causing nervousness in the financial markets.
About half of economists recently surveyed by Blomberg anticipated a rate hike this week.
However, financial markets expect the Fed to delay the matter, with a key indicator of only about a 30% chance of an increase.
This would point to a decline in the stock market if the Fed raises rates only if politicians soften the slump by promising that a further increase is in sight.
On Tuesday, the Dow Jones industrial average jumped 228.89 points, or 1.4%, to 16 599.85.
The rally was triggered by expectations that Fed policymakers would delay rates this week calm, says John Lonski, chief economist at Moody's Capital Markets Research Group.
"It would be a surprise if the Fed were to raise rates now," he states.
If Fed politicians did this, "they'd have to go all the way in their programme statement and hold hands on the financial markets so they don't panic."
Other experts have argued that raising rates would appease financial markets because it would remove uncertainty about how the Fed will proceed.
Just a month ago, the Fed appeared to be well on its way to a 0.25 percentage-point rate hike this month, the first small step in a slow tightening of monetary conditions that would confirm the strength of the U.S. economic recovery.
Yellen said she expects a rate hike this year despite complaints from liberals that more economic progress is needed despite complaints from liberals.
Although job creation slowed in August, the labour market has seen solid growth this year.
The unemployment rate fell to 5.1% in the previous month, the lowest in more than seven years and is close to what the Fed considers full employment.
Wage gains showed signs of lifting.
And although inflation continues to take place below the central bank's annual 2% target, Fed politicians argue that the main reason was the temporary effect of significantly lower oil prices.
The economy "can cope with higher rates and needs them, given the tightness in the labor market," said Ian Shepherdson, chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics.
But financial markets around the world began towing at the end of last month after China devalued its currency.
Dow Jones' industrial average fell as much as 16% below its record in May, when investors feared that China's slowing economy would drag down global growth.
The market has bounced somewhat this month and the Dow is now losing only nearly 10% of the May high.
The Fed's uneasy market situation adds a comprehensive calculus to start raising the interest rate.
"In the absence of what has happened in the financial markets, and reports on the weakness of the Chinese economy over the past few weeks, there would be a tightening at this meeting," said Stephen D. Oliner, senior researcher at UCLA Ziman Center for Real Estate and former Fed official.
They are now predicting to wait at least a month to gain better control of the consequences.
"There's a lot of uncertainty about the impact it's going to have on the U.S. economy, and they don't have nearly any data to rely on about China's latest developments," Oliner said.
The state of health of the global economy is not one of the Fed's main interests, so technically it should not be included in their decision-making.
But in reality, the Fed has become the monetary authority of the world.
The central bank's influences in recent years have been enormous.
A mere suggestion by then-President Ben S. Bernanke in 2013 that the Fed could "tighten" their monetary expansion in the market has triggered cramps around the world.
In a world where markets and economies are increasingly interconnected, Fed officials can be expected to monitor developments elsewhere, but also consult with other central bankers and experts so that they can better read the state of the global economy.
Their decision-making process is "more art than science," said Zachary Karabell, head of Envestnet Inc's global wealth-management strategy.
Jeffrey Lacker, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, Va., noted this month that "the Fed has a history of exaggerated movements in financial markets that appear incoherent with economic fundamentals."
Lacker, a voting member of the Federal Open-Market's policy-making that pushed for a rate hike, cited the 1998-99 decision as an example.
The financial crisis in Asia has led the Fed to reduce its reference rate by 0.75 percentage points during three meetings "Despite the limited identifiable impacts on growth in the U.S.," Lacker said.
A year later, Fed officials reversed those cuts.
The Fed should prioritise concerns about financial stability only during a major crisis, such as the 2008 market collapse, said Adam Posen S., a former member of a committee setting rates at the Bank of England.
The latest turmoil doesn't even approach this level, he said.
“You certainly don’t want to be in a situation of fear of rising rates, because the markets are upset,” said Posen, president of the Institute for International Economics Peterson.
The Federal Reserve Act still has a double mandate and doesn't mention financial stability, and until that changes, I don't think they should get to the top.
Do you need a great Thai market?
Try LAX-C in Chinatown.
Yes, Chinatown
The market/warehouse was opened in 1997 and is owned by LAX-C Inc.
Often referred to as Thai Costco, LAX-C is a wholesale Thai market that also sells to the public.
Yes, it's a Chinatown and not a Thai neighborhood.
The market/warehouse is the only point of contact that offers a stunning array of fresh, frozen and dried goods, popular Thai and Asian snacks, and kitchen utensiles, furniture, religious and festive decorations.
There's even an automobile part available if you need a bit of engine oil.
The store is massive and it will take you over an hour to crawl through all the aisles.
If you're hungry about all this shopping, go for a meal at the LAX-C BBQ Express.
The internal trading booth (good, cheap) is close to the registers and has various sampling points.
What's in stock: Do you need something - anything - Thai?
You'll probably find it in LAX-C.
Items are affordable, and a lot of items are sold in bulk.
The limit of fresh produce belongs to special Asian and regional Thai herbs, fruits and more.
The expansion of meat is great, for example fish - the employees behind the counter will wash you and body it, even fry it if you wish,
A good part of the store is left to a massive freezer compartment, where you'll find frozen curry leaves, bitter watermelon and galangal, whole ducks, fish, beef blood and bile, pork intestines, fish balls, regional sausages, over-the-counter semi-finished products and more.
Who buys here: Owners of restaurants and chefs, also separate families and individuals.
Wide aisles meet shopping trolleys and forklifts.
Find: Fresh monong durian next to the checkout stand.
Where it's located: The store is in an industrial part of Los Angeles, surrounding the suburbs of Chinne's neighborhood and downtown - a few blocks from Philippe The Original.
Parking: LAX-C has its own car park, which it shares with other shops and restaurants.
While the store can be occupied, parking is usually not hard to find.
The speed on the highways will stay the same.
Acceleration to 150 km/h was rejected by the House.
It will not be possible to increase the maximum speed limit up to 150 km/h under favorable conditions on selected sections of motorways.
On Tuesday, the House made the decision when it acceded to an amendment by the Senate, which declined to increase speed.
An amendment to the Roads Act will now reach the president.
President Miloš Zeman said at the end of April that he would veto the law allowing fifty-kilometre speed.
Dan Ťok, Minister of Transport (for ANO), was also opposed to the increase, according to which the Czech motorway network is not yet built for such speed.
Ťok backed a Senate version of the bill on Tuesday before MPs.
The approved amendment will now allow, in accordance with the government's original proposal, an increase in speeds from 90 to 110 kilometres per hour on selected first-class roads, in particular four-lane.
At first, the Chamber extended this possibility to highways, on which it would be possible to ride in selected sections at speeds of up to 150 km/h, but the Senate rejected it.
MPs have now accepted his proposal.
Senators, with the consent of the Minister of Transport from the amendment, have also exempted the authorization of the traffic police to have the last spole when modifying the local roads taken care of by municipalities.
The senate version approved by the House also envisages that sewerage, water supply or public lighting cables can be stored longitudinally below the road surface.
Municipalities would thus save for the purchase of land.
Municipalities should also not be obliged to take care of road vegetation along local roads, regardless of the owner of the land.
The amendment is also intended to help improve the protection of roads and to make it easier for municipalities to handle towed vehicles to which owners do not subscribe.
Newly, municipalities could sell towed vehicles, about which their owners do not apply within a six-month period of notification of towing.
Municipalities will have the opportunity to exclude transit freight traffic through their territory on second- and third-class roads under the threat of a fine even for forwarding firms.
The amendment also abolishes the possibility to introduce electronic vignettes instead of existing paper stamps, which will also be used after 1 January next year.
According to the government, this will save money for the introduction of an electronic system.
Ustian ex-prime minister goes on trial.
He's facing 10 years for overpriced citizen welcome.
The case relates to an allegedly overpriced project of welcoming citizens.
Police also wanted to prosecute MP Zuzana Kailová (ČSSD) in this case, but the Chamber of Deputies did not issue it.
According to the legal qualifications of the case, both face up to
The trial is currently scheduled for two days in October.
According to state prosecutor Mandík and Macík caused at least one million crowns in damages, according to the indictment.
The plaintiff relies on an expert opinion.
Former members of the city's management, according to the plaintiff by virtue of their office, unlawfully allowed it to obtain the property benefit of the company, which organized the welcoming of the citizes.
According to the indictment, Mandik ordered the then head of the Municipal Department of Urban Services, Education and Culture to address five interconnected firms in the selection process.
When another company that offered the lowest prize entered the competition, the town hall canceled the competition.
The contract was then to be entrusted by the city to the administrator and only Darothore entered into the competition, which was then awarded by the City Council.
The city paid out at least a million extra.
The two accusations led by the aim of securing for Darothore the award of a public contract for the organisation of the Welcome Citizens event in Ústí nad Labem for a long and coordinated manner have acted at the least in violation of the requirement to proceed with the care of a proper manager, writes the prosecutor.
At the same time, according to him, they were aware that Darothore will subsequently, after obtaining this contract, charge Ústí nad Labem for the implementation of the Welcome of Citizens event, which will be disproportionately increased compared to the fair value of the services actually provided.
In three years, according to the file, the company collected more than five million crowns for the project.
In the end, the city paid only one part, about 1.7 million crowns, then withdrew from the contract.
According to the expert opinion, the paid amount was at least one million crowns overpriced.
Mandik has previously denied any violation of the law.
I didn't commit any crime, I'm innocent.
I don't understand this fabricated case, the former mayor said earlier, adding that the city under his leadership only followed up on a similar project from previous years.
Between 2008 and 2010, the event was provided by Roxy II.
"It was under the same conditions, for the same money," Mandik added.
The criminals originally wanted to indict MP Kail, who was also a member of the city council.
However, the Chamber of Deputies did not release it for prosecution.
Criminals have therefore postponed the case in her case, but are counting on Kailová to be charged after her parliamentary mandate expires.
Welcoming citizens was a social event that took place several times a year.
Mothers received a gift cheque for 5 thousand crowns for the purchase of children's supplies and small gifts.
Hungarian police officers allegedly released a dog on a fugitive.
Then they prompted the reporter to delete the footage.
Hungarian police are said to have briefly detained an AP cameraman on the border with Serbia, forcing him to delete the footage on which a police dog allegedly knocked a fugitive to the ground.
However, the Hungarian government disputes these accusations.
The alleged incident happened on Saturday night when AP cameraman Luca Muzi filmed migrants across a cornfield near the Hungarian city of Röszke to cross the border with Serbia.
Officers intervened against the fugitives, and one of them had a certain Syrian attack a dog with a basket on his muzzle.
The dog of the man who shouted "Please, please, I'm a fugitive," said Muzi.
One of the cops noticed that Muzi was filming everything and did not allow him to leave.
The journalist was no longer allowed to call either his superiors or colleagues on the spot.
Muzi's officers took Muzi to a dark place near the refugee registration centre and asked him to show them what he's recorded.
Then they ordered him to delete the footage.
Muzi thus lost the result of a two-day work in Serbia and Hungary.
He says he felt in danger because there were dogs nearby.
The Associated Press has sent a protest letter to the Hungarian government's spokesperson, Zoltán Kovács.
She described the behavior of police officers as unacceptable and said that journalists should be able to cover events without risking physical violence and not having to fear that the government would destroy legally acquired material.
Kovács, citing a police report, questioned Muzi's account of the events.
He said the officers just wanted a journalist to show them if they were in some of the footage, to which they were said to be entitled.
When they found out they weren't there, they didn't force him to grease anything.
"Dogs can bark - they're trained to deter and scare a little bit, but they didn't attack with certainty," Kovács argues.
