Jarryd Hayne had several options to start his NFL career.
Detroit and Seattle showed a lot of interest after his move to the United States, and a lot of teams questioned after his great start to practice at the end of last year.
But Hayne chose the San Francisco 49ers and coach Jim Tomsul, and it looks like a reasonable decision.
Some coaches let Hayne sit for the rest of the season because he made a mistake, which was done by an Australian rugbie league convert during his first kick-off from the pitch during Tuesday's 20:3 win at Minnesota.
But Tomsula has showned that he has confidence in the players and has given him another chance to come back and cast him in the game after Reggie Bush's injury.
Tomsula also continued to be sincere in the locker room after the game.
Instead of chewing the rookies in front of the rest of the team, he was able to encourage them.
Tomsula also supported Hayne publicly at a press conference after the game.
Reporter 49ers, Cam Inman, said Hayne had also received encouragement from a special team coordinator, Thomas McGaughey, who advised him to forget the mistake and only care about the next game.
