4 people killed after officers fired rubber bullets at Nepal protesters
Four people, including a four-year-old boy, were killed in southern Nepal after police fired gumbling projectiles into a crowd of protesters against a proposal for a new state constitution, an official said Wednesday.
Violence occurred on Tuesday night in the Rupandehi district, about 175 miles west of Kathmandu's capital.
Bishnu Prasad Dhakal, a senior county officer, says more than 1,000 protesters gathered in front of a police station and some threw rocks and incendiary bombs.
Mr Dhakal reports that police used tear gas and then fired into the air before resorting to rubber projectiles.
Twenty police officers and five civilians have been wounded, he says.
It wasn't immediately clear why rubber projectiles, which are known for their ability to kill but are traditionally used as a less lethal alternative, have apparently caused a high number of deaths.
The clash was the last in a week of violent demonstrations across the southern plain of Nepal, where members of Madhesi's ethnic group say they have set up a new province under the proposed new constitution, and has tempered their political influence.
Further demonstrations in western Nepal have also been recorded by the Tharus ethnic group, which calls for the establishment of its own state.
Since August 10, at least 40 people have been killed in violent clashes, including 11 police officers.
when the four main political parties signed an agreement on the division of the country into the provinces – a big step towards a new constitution, but a very contentious issue in Nepal, which has been centrally managed for centuries.
Labors on the new constitution have persisted since 2008, but lawmakers have accelerated the trial after a devastating earthquake that killed thousands of people in April.
Legislators voted this week in Kathmandu on establishing a draft constitution, and officials say the new charter should be adopted by Sunday.
