The NHC predicts the 60 percent probability of cyclone south of the Cape Verde Islands
A broad low-pressure system of about 350 miles (560 km) south of Cape Verde's southernmost has a probability of 60 percent in the formation of a tropical cyclone in the next 48 hours, the U.S. National Hurricane Center said Tuesday.
Another well-defined low-pressure system around the middle between the Cape Verde Islands and the Small Antilles also has a 60-per cent chance of forming a tropical cyclone formation over the next two days, the Miami-based weather forecaster added.
Google has launched a campaign to collect $11 million for refugees.
Google Inc (GOOGL.O) said it is launching a campaign that aims to collect $11 million for humanitarian organizations helping thousands of refugees who have flooded European states when fleeing war-torn and poor countries. Google will double each amount collected.
Google has announced this on its blog in a very unusual way.
The message was posted on her blog by Google employee Rita Masoud, based on her personal experience, when she fled with her family from Kabul as a seven-year-old.
“We’ve travelered on many dark trains and buses, and we’ve experienced hunger, thirst, winter, and fear,” Masoud said.
Well, I was lucky.
But with the deteriorating immigration crisis in Europe, more and more families, like mine, are willing to help.
All contributions will be distributed among 4 non-profit organizations helping refugees and immigrants: Doctors Without Borders, the International Emergency Committee, Save the Children and the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees.
Google said it will double all posts made through google.com/refugeerelief up to $5.5 million.
Europe is facing the largest wave of refugees since the Second World War. Most of them come from Syria, tossed by a four-year war, which this year alone has driven to 4 million people.
Many people are also fleeing from war-torn Afghanistan, Yemen, Iraq and Libya.
Yasmeen Abutaleb; Editing: Christian Plumb
For slightly obese diabetics, weight loss surgery could be useful
The benefits of weight loss surgery for slightly obese people with type 2 diabetes can last for at least five years, according to a new study.
However, it is still too early to say whether slightly obese people with type 2 diabetes live longer after weight loss surgery than those who are given non-surgical treatment.
"Mortality data takes a long time to display," argues Dr. Robin Blackstone, a weight-loss surgery expert who wrote an editorial on JAMA's new study.
Surgeries for weight loss, bariatric surgery use various methods to shrink the stomach.
Reducing hunger and limiting the human body to absorb food.
Over the years, bariatric operations have proved effective for the treatment of type 2 diabetes, but most of the survey was conducted on people with morbid obesity with a body mass index (BMI) of 25 or higher.
BMī, which measures the relationship of weight relative to height, is considered normal in values between 18.5 and 24.9.
BMī values of 25 or higher indicate overweight of a person, and people with values above 30 are considered obese.
You can calculate your BMI here: 1.usa.gov/1D0ZqDv
For their new studies, researchers at Min-Shengh General Hospital in Taiwan have used data collected from a study since 2007, which deals with two types of bariatric surgery - gastric bypass and gastrectomies - in type 3 diabetes treatments in people who were slightly obese.
The average BMI among those who underwent surgery dropped from 31 to 24.5 at the end of their fifth year, according to the study.
In the meantime, BMīs who were not given surgical treatment for diabetes remained roughly the same at 29.
Among patients with surgery, diabetes has been resolved completely in 36 percent and partly in 28 cases.
In the drug-treated group, it was resolved completely in only 1 percent for comparison of diabetes and partially only in about 2 percent of patients.
In addition, control of blood pressure, trigylcerides and "bad" LDL cholesterol was also proven "generally better in the surgical group," the authors found.
The researchers also monitored the patient's hemoglobin A1c levels in his blood, which reflects ongoing blood sugar levels in the body.
Hemoglobin A1c is best kept below 7 percent, according to researchers.
After the operation, the average hemoglobin A1c fell from about 9 percent to about 6 percent.
However, in drug-treated groups, this value remained at about 8 percent.
But this better "glycemic control" - reflected in better levels of hemoglobin A1c - has not reduced mortality in five years, according to chief researcher Dr. Chih-Cheng Hsu and his colleagues.
The research team also compared the average results with two types of operations.
After the fifth year, a group with bypass lost more weight compared to the group gastrectomy (18.7 versus 14.2 kg), achieved greater BMI declines (7.4 against 5.1) and hemoglobin A1c (3.1 percent against 2.1 percent) and were more likely to have diabetes remission (46.9 percent versus 16.7 percent).
While the new study does not show an improvement in survival or surgery after five years, there is evidence from the study of stronger people in Sweden, where they have found that surgery leads to an extension of life, says Blackstone of the University of Arizona College or Medicine-Phoenix.
According to a Swedish study, obese people who underwent bariatric surgery had about a 29 percent greater chance of survival over the next 15 years, compared to a group that tried more conventional methods.
Although the new study has not showned an improvement in survival among patients after surgery, according to Blackstone, this is a reassuring outcome for people with a BMI below 35 who are interested in these procedures.
"Right here, this work is critical because it states that surgery is safer in a lower BMI group," without increasing the risk of death or kidney disease, he states.
Blackstone also further argues that people and their doctors should start taking weight really seriously after a decrease in the patient's BMI between 27 and 30.
"I think we've been waiting too long to show people the gravity of the situation," he says.
I think that once the build-up of these genetic changes, which firmly connect their bodies to obesity and diabetes, the way back is already really hard.
"It's impossible for everyone to come to Europe" - Dalai Lama - RT News
Europe is unable to accommodate all refugees, Dalai Lama said, adding that a true solution to the current refugee crisis lies in the Middle East.
The Tibetan Buddhist leader has called for an end to violence in refugee home countries.
The Dalai Lama addressed the issue of the ongoing refugee crisis in his speech at the opening of the Dalai Lama Centre for Compassion in Oxford, which deals with an ethics study, at the beginning of his 10-day visit to Great Britain.
He argued that, under current circumstances, humanity's interest should be placed before the interests of individual countries or even continents.
He also stressed that the response of Western European countries, however admirable, to the refugee crisis was inadequate in this situation.
"It's impossible for everyone to come to Europe," he said in his speech.
Although Dalai Lama praised the efforts of Germany and Austria to resolve the crisis, he stressed that this was only a temporary solution.
"Taking care of several thousand refugees is wonderful, but in the meantime, you'll have to think about a long-term solution to bring real peace and real development, especially through the education of these Muslim countries," he stressed.
Ultimately, we have to think about how to reduce killings in their countries.
We have to limit the use of force.
The use of force has never solved these problems," he added.
In dealing with violence issues, Dalai Lama has also commented on lawsuits against George Bush after the 9/11 terrorist attack, claiming that "a violent U.S. response has sparked a chain of uncontrollable events.
After 9/11, I expressed my condolences in a letter to President Bush, advising him that the way to solve this problem was not a violent path.
I know his motives were good, but he used force and created unexpected consequences," the Dalai Lama said.
A quadruple increase in the number of children ingested by the ambulance in the last 4 years
Toxicology centers around the United States have reported a nearly 400-percentage increase since 2010 in the number of children under the age of 12, swallowed by a highly alcoholic sanitation agent, according to a toxicology center in Georgia.
According to Dr Gaylord Lopez, director of the toxicology center of Georgia, the number of ingestions of sanitation in children under the age of 12 who were reported to poison control centers increased from 3 266 in 2010 to 16 117 in 2014.
"Children are getting to these products more often, and unfortunately, there's a percentage of children who have to be taken to the emergency room," Lopez told CNN.
Some kids drink sanitizer intentionally, while some want to impress their friends or as a challenge on social media.
Youtube videos show teenagers drinking sanitation because of cheap fuss.
The teenagers reportedly mixed a sanitating agent with a mouthwash containing alcohol for a stronger drink.
Younger children can be attracted by the attractive scent of the remedy.
"Children don't think it could hurt them," Lopez said.
Many more attractive (sanitation products) are those that are perfumed.
There are sanitations with the scent of strawberry, grapefruit or orange, which are very tempting for children.
Sanitation agent contains between 45 and 95 percent of alcohol.
Especially in young children, a few cubs can indicate alcohol poisoning.
"This is a highly concentrated alcohol," Dr. Stephen Thornton, medical director at the University of Kansas Hospital, told Fox 4 in Kansas City.
You wouldn’t let a glass of whiskey hang out either, but people leave these sanitations outdoors, and when children get to them, it’s a quick way to consuming alcohol.
Nhaijah Russel, a six-year-old girl who recently ingested up to four sips of a sanitary agent at school with the smell of strawberry and was taken to the emergency room for treatment.
Her blood alcohol level was .179, which is twice the threshold considered to be intoxicated in an adult, according to Dr Chris Ritchey, an emergency doctor who took care of her at Gwinnett Medical Center outside Atlanta.
Nhaijah uttered indistinctible words and was unable to walk after arriving at the emergency room.
Doctors monitored her overnight at a separate children's hospital for any signs of brain trauma because the girl fell due to alcohol and punched herself in the head, Ritchey said.
"It's been very scary," Ortoria Scott, Nhaijah's mother, told CNN.
It could have been deadly to my child.
Lopez advised parents and teachers the use of non-alcoholic products or to arrange for sanitation supplies to be out of the reach of children.
In addition to alcohol poisoning, some sanitations were associated with death.
In 2013, two Ontaria women died after swallowing a sanitation agent containing a toxic, undeclared ingredient.
Health professionals considered that the product contained methanol, a deadly reagent, rather than ethanol, which was listed as an active ingredient.
In January, three fourth-year students in northern New York planned to poison their "evil" teacher by deploying antibacterial products around the room," according to a police report.
The teacher is very allergic to sanitation devices and has banned their presence in the room.
Police assessed the thwarted plan as "innocence chatter," and referred to the discipline of the school district,
