Scotland's unemployment remains unchanged, while overall U.K. unemployment rises
UNEMPLOYMENCE in Scotland remains unchanged at 164 000 between May and July, while the total number of unemployees in the UK has risened by 10,000 to 1.82 million over the same period.
Scotland's 5.9 per cent unemployment rate was above the U.K. rate (5.5 percent), as data from the Office for National Statistics (ONS) have shown.
Meanwhile, employment in Scotland fell by 12,000 over a period of three months, the number of workers has now halted to 2 612 000.
Scotland's employment rate fell to 74.0 per cent during the quarter - a number above Britain's average of 73.5 per cent.
David Mundell, Scotland's secretary of state, said in commentary on the latest figures: "The long-term government's plan laid the foundations for a stronger economy."
It's been almost exactly a year since Scotland made a historic decision to remain part of the UK.
In these 12 months, we have witnessed further improvements in the labour market, and Scottish employment has risen, while unemployment has declined.
Why did I witness the execution in Oklahoma
Later today, despite late legal intervention, I'll be watching a man's death.
If that happens, and I hope it doesn't happen, I'll sit in a viewing gallery in Oklahom State Prison's chamber of death and see Richard Glossip executed by lethal injection.
He asked me to be one of the four officials who will witness his death.
I first received the invitation in January through my best friend and although I was shocked, I agreed.
As the day approaches, I'm starting to be afraid of it.
Then why am I doing this?
I've accepted this invitation, because I think it's a good way I'll be able to tell you the entire death penalty story.
It would give me a unique perspective.
It's true that journalists are allowed to pursue executions in America, and because there are often more requests than vacancies, a morbid lottery is carried out to decide who will be elected.
Oklahoma has reduced the number of places for journalists from 12 to five.
Whoever is selected will watch the entire execution unbiased.
That's what I can't do.
I'll sit alongside Richard Glossip's closest friends and I've created a strange bond with him through months of phone calls and one two-and-a-half-hour visit.
I like this boy.
We often laugh when we talk.
The prison administration says I can't be a witness and a journalist at the same time, so I won't be allowed a notebook and I won't be able to record what I see.
I'll just have to memorize all the details.
One Oklahoma journalist accused me on Twitter of compromising my impartiality.
It's a lesson.
Tell the story of the inmate in the way he likes it, get access.
I've complained to her.
She conceded that "she could have articulated it better," but she still thinks what I'm doing is wrong.
Richard Glossip had a problem with some of the things I said and wrote.
He doesn't tell me what to write, and even if he did, I wouldn't listen.
I told him he could remove me from the witness list at any time.
However, he wants an international journalist ready to tell the story.
He thinks if he dies, his case will help procedures against the death penalty.
Kim Bellware, a journalist from the Huffington Post, will be by my side.
If you want to know more information about the case, please listen to a series of podcasts that I have created.
I won't repeat all the details here again.
I can't say with certainty that Richard Glossip is innocent.
His actions following the murder of Barry Van Trees would make him guilty of complicity in a crime.
But I believe there's a good chance he's innocent of murder.
I certainly believe there has not been enough evidence to justify the death penalty.
In my opinion, execution is bad.
And it is from this perspective that I will follow his death.
I can't say I'll be really objective.
For this purpose, you can contact other journalists.
What you'll get from me is the experience of seeing the person I love, to die an unnecessary death.
Man escaped from Canning River after police chase
