Mood, best known for preparing the undefeated Black Caviar champion, described the rule of treatment on the day of the race as "ridiculous" after a clay compress or mud was applied to the horse's feet, which is against the rules.
It's against the rules of racing.
I have to take full responsibility for that," he said.
Someone on my team accidentally put mud on his leg.
He could've had it last night.
It could have happened yesterday, and it happened.
It happened on the day of the race.
Moody faced a three-month disqualification for the treatment of compresses on the day of the race, to which organizers had already opened an investigation.
It's a joke.
The people who create these rules don't know anything about horses.
"We all understand that we need the rules, but this has gone too far," Moody said.
Cobram driver who escaped was arrested
Police have arrested a man who hit a cyclist in Cobram over the weekend and drove off after a collision.
The cyclist was airlifted to the Royal Melbourne Hospital with serious injuries after he was hitted by a car on Murray Valley's main road Saturday morning.
Police said the motorist drove off without stopping and helping the injured rider.
On Monday, a 50-year-old Nathalia man himself confessed to Shepparton police.
He is expected to be charged with failing to stop in an accident, failure to provide assistance and other traffic violations.
The cyclist, a 40-year-old man from Cobram, remains in hospital in stable condition.
Melbourne Storm coach Craig Bellamy strikes back at rivals over wrestling claims
Craig Bellamy, the Melbourne Storm coach, criticized his players' interventions as "setting a plan" by two NRL favourites of the premiership.
Trent Robinson, the Sydney Roosters coach, said referees had allowed Storm players to "wrest" his players during the Storm's controversial victory in the Sydney qualifying final on Friday night.
Wayne Bennet, the Brisbane Broncos coach, made a slightly veiled note on Strorm after his team won qualifiers over the North Queensland Cowboys on Saturday night when he called the game as a "parade" of the rugby league and, according to him, two from Queensland weren't "too big" on wrestling.
Bellamy defended his players' interventions and said they were concentrating on "contact" during them.
Bellamy added that this situation boosted his self-confidencence because his team is criticized by two leading coaches, which may mean they are fearful of defeat from the Storm team.
"It's still coming - I don't remember Trent Robinson talking about wrestling when they beat us 24-2," Bellamy said Wednesday.
I can guarantee that in the last year we haven't changed anything from our defensive system and techniques, we've only put the emphasis on contact during procedures over the last year, and that's meant a turnaround for us.
Trent has been here for three years now and he's obviously coaching a great team, so he's probably not used to losing, which could have been a bit of a shock for him.
It's funny how such things always come in this year's part.
Bellamy devalued Bennet's idea of "exciting football" and reminded his mentor that Bennetov's St George-Illawarra Dragons team won the 2010 premiership by playing "boring" football.
Waney's talking about exciting football.
He was right in that his Saturday's game was as exciting as ours. It was also just as exciting," Bellamy stated.
I remember in 2010 St George was criticized for being a boring team and Wayne then said he didn't really care.
But now he has a team that manages to play the style of the game he finds cancelled, especially his young midfielders with their speed.
You'd like to play that kind of football if you had this type of players at your disposal.
He said that opinion in 2010, and now he wants all teams to play his style of football - but not every team has two midfielders like Anthony Milford and Ben Hunt, so they can't play that style.
It inspires more self-confidence in me because they say things like that because they're worried about the match against us.
Six weeks ago, they probably didn't even think about us when they got into big games.
Bellamy also pointed to the fact that there were more wickets in the Storm and Roosters match than in the Broncos and Cowboys match.
The Broncos team's game was probably good, I didn't look, but what's the score? 16-12?
Our game ended 20-18, there were two more attempts in our game, but it's exciting football?
Or are breaks and not scoring attempts interesting?
It's an agenda.
If they mention us, it means that we will definitely meet again at the stage.
Storm have a break this weekend and have won the right to host either the North Queensland Cownboys or the Cronulla Sharks in the preliminary final next Saturday night at AAMI Park. Tickets will go on sale from morning next Tuesday.
Serbian man receives US$200000 bail for drug charges
A man who faced a possible life imprisonment for possessing 40 kilograms of cocaine that was smuggled from Queensland was bailed at US$200,000.
Marko Maksimovic (29) was one of five men who were arrested last month by federal police searching for 100-kilox drug from a Solay yacht that arrived from South America through Vanuat and docked at Coomera in August.
Maksimovic was spotted during a meeting with the accused together at the port during the morning and later met them at Coomera Roadhouse, where police found 40 kilograms of the drug in the Toyota Echo.
Even though he faced a potential verdicular life sentence for owning cocaine of commercial quality, Maksimovice's lawyer based his defense on the fact that there was no evidence that he owned the whole quantity and that he might have been at the cafe quite by accident and innocence.
Supreme Court Justice Peter Flanagan believed Maksimov would flee.
It has a clear connection to Serbia.
He often travels to Serbia," he told the court on Wednesday.
In the crown case, he lied to the police about where he lived.
The mother and stepfather of Maksimovice jointly offered a deposit of USD 200 000 for his release.
"After reading the affidavited statements of both persons, it is clear that the deposit of USD 200 000 is a real financial burden for them," Judge Flanagan said.
Their efforts alone were clearly insufficient because the accused did not appear further in court, he warned.
Maksimovic has been granted bail on the condition that he surrenders both Australian and Serbian passports, reports to the police daily, and adheres to curfews from 18 o'clock in the evening to 6 a.m.
Stevan Hogg used the girl's phone to lure paedophiles to send pictures
The pedophile used the eleven-year-old girl's phone to retrieve photos of child abuse.
Stevan Hogg took the girl's phone when she was asleep, then logged on to her online chat, where he had a lot of indecent photos of children sent to him by another user.
The girl then woke up and found that her phone was missing and then she found him in the hand of the sleeping Hogg.
She went through it and, before alerting her mother, found disturbing news.
Police became involved and saw five photos, then analytical computers found the address and discovered a "worrying" search on the web using expressions to search for indecent images of children.
Hogg then told officers that his reason was to "guide the paedophiles" on the Internet before "pretending to be a police officer and scaring them."
Fiscal representative Eilidh Robertson told Dundee Sheriff Court: "He said it's an addiction - that he's addicted to scaring people.
He confessed that he did the search on the computer, but claims that he just wanted to pretend he was a police officer to scare paedophiles.
He was asked about his conversation with an unknown person who sent him pictures and why he wanted her to send more.
According to him, he did it to lure them
He says he skipped drunk and said he wanted to see pictures of rapes and homicides.
Ms. Robertson added: "When the girl found messages on her phone, he called her mother, shaking and crying.
She saw naughty pictures on her phone and the defendant was then confronted.
The girl was interviewed and talked about how the defendant was using her computer and Playstation so that he could talk to girls who looked much younger than him.
Hog, 23, residing in Ward Road, Dundee, pleaded guilty to filing an accusation to accept or provide inappropriate images of children on June 14 last year, violating the terms of bail on January 25 and failed to appear in court on March 24 this year.
Advocate attorney Gregor Sim asked that Hogg not be placed on the sex offender list because he can argue that there was "no essential sexual subtext" in his act.
Sheriff Alistair Carmichael postponed his sentence for October for filing reports to social workers.
Hogg was granted bail in this case, but was held in custody before a hearing on other unresolved cases.
He was placed on a list of sex offenders before the date of his sentence.
Zimbawsky's Robert Mugabe delivered a bad speech in parliament
Mr Mugabe, Africa's oldest leader, earlier this year fell on the steps leading to the podium.
He wasn't injured, but the video of his fall went viral on social media.
The opening parliament has also been tainted by claims by opposition lawmakers that they have received anonymous threats of manslaughter warning them against boosing Mr Mugabe during his speech.
He was booed last month and provoked during his speech on the state of the nation in parliament - a speech he reiterated on Tuesday.
The chairman of the Movement for Democrative Change (MDC), Innocent Gones, said seven opposition politicians received text messages on their mobile phones with a warning not to obstruct Mr Mugabe's speech.
"The report comes from a number that doesn't appear, but is named 'death'," Mr. Mugabe told reporters.
It warns members in question to know that immunity ends up in parliament and, as soon as it comes out of parliament, their parliamentary immunity doesn't work.
The chief said the party led by Morgan Tsvangirai has "fears" over threats against MPs.
Hewlett-Packard is preparing to reduce the number of jobs by up to 30,000.
Hewlett-Packard canceled between 25 000 and 30,000 jobs as part of its efforts to restructure and save the company's commercial services.
Later this year, HP will split into two listed companies, separating the business of computers and printers from its faster-growing corporate hardware and service operations.
The anticipated job cut will result in a change of about £2.7 billion, and the start will occur in the fourth quarter, HP said in a statement.
"These restructuring activities will allow us to create a more competitive structure with sustainable spending for the new Hewlett Packard Enterprise," says Meg Whitman, HP's chief executive officer and chairman of the board of directors, who will be at the forefront of the unit after the split.
"Hewlett Packard Enterprise will be smaller and more sophisticated than HP today and will have a broad and extensive portfolio of stores that will help businesses transition to a new style of business," Whitman said.
As a separate company, we will be in a much better position to meet the evolving requirements of our customers around the world.
Hewlett Parckard Enterprise will, according to the company's statement, have an annual income of more than $50 billion and "will be focused on providing unrivalled integrated technology solutions" to companies.
This will lead to the layout of a company founded in 1930 by Stanford University graduates Bill Hewlett and Dave Packard, producing electrical equipment and whose garage in Palo Alto was nicknamed the "Birthplace of Silicon Valley."
HP has undergone a massive reorganization to manage the transfer from traditional personal computers to mobile devices.
This move by HP, the second-largest PC manufacturer and one of America's largest technical firms, is the latest in this sector based on the belief that a narrowly specialized firm has better results.
Arsenal captain Mikel Arteta says team-mates have to get behind injured Jack Wilshere before Dinamo Zagreb
Wenger also emphasized to Wilshere that he's still September, and if his rehabilitation goes as planned, there's no reason he won't play again at Christmas.
This best scenario already means Wilshere will surely miss at least another four English games and, from the perspective of history, his involvement in the next summer European Championship is clearly uncertain.
The big pity of this latest problem is that Wilshere ended last season strong after an ankle injury and then got back into former form during the pre-season.
"This kind of injury doesn't jeopardize a career," Wenger argues.
It's a wound that requires healing.
It's nothing special.
It was just an accident.
It didn't concern his ankles or the injuries he had sustained before.
This could be true from a purely medical point of view, as Wenger acknowledged that there is a relative danger of further injuries whenever any player returns during the season.
There is also an unpleasant pattern.
A survey conducted by Premier Injuries Ltd. has recorded Wilshera's 23 different injuries since he made his gaming debut six years ago.
Over the past five years, he has only played in Arsenal's 65 league games out of a total of 157:
From a broader perspective for the Arsenal, this means that in six out of eight seasons since 2007-08 he has been one of the two Premier League clubs who have lost the most days due to injuries.
Wenger accepts the potential of a vicious circle, but Arsenal believes the improvement in the area of recorded injuries over last season will continue.
“Sometimes when you’re coming back after an injury, you’re having a vulnerable period, a time when you need to strengthen your body and get competition.”
Jack's young enough to get out.
I hope his body stabilizes, I'm sure he does and he's going to have a career he deserves.
Arsenal begin the campaign in the Champions League on Wednesday night against Dinamo Zagreb matches with Chelsea on Saturday and Tottenham Hotspur next Wednesday, and Wenger prepares to rotate his team.
Héctor Bellerín and Aaron Ramsey did not travel to Croatia, that is, Mathieu Debuchy and Arteta are likely to enter.
Wenger could also start with Kieran Gibbs and Olivier Giroud instead of Nacho Monreal Theo Walcot.
"I'm trying to maintain my balance and let the players who need it exhale," Wenger said.
Although Arsenal is the clear favourite to advance from Group F, Maksimir in Zagreb has a reputation as Europe's most intimidating stadium.
Dinamo, too, were not beaten in a total of 41 games after retaining their "unbeatable" campaign last season.
The stadium shuddered on Wednesday night with an earthquake of 3.2 degrees Richtera.
That was the 10th time in Zagreb this year.
Wenger thinks the Arsenal have learned their lesson from last year's Champions League season, capitulating against Monaco and he remains far removed from the hope of leading the club to its first European Cup victory.
"This commitment is tremendous," Wenger argues.
It never happened to Arsenal.
In 2006 we were very close, but on the other hand I have to be realistic.
We're ambitious, but we're not dreamers.
FriendsFest: a comedy show that gives us serious lessons about male friendship
The absence of similar depictions in various shows is blatant, especially given the need for men to open up more desperate than ever when it comes to suicides among young men under 15.
In 2013, it was the only biggest cause of death in men aged 20-45 in the UK and remains three times more common in men than in women.
In an effort to address the issue, Campaign Against Living Miserably (CALM) marked 2015 as the Year of the Man, which aims to challenge a culture that prevents men from seeking help if necessary.
At the heart of this struggle lies a friendship for the fulfillment of this challenge.
A recent study of more than 2000 high school students showing symptoms of depression found that in the event that the injured person had a sufficiently high percentage of friends with a "healthy mood," their chances of recovery doubled.
Likewise, for those who are not depressed, a circle of mentally healthy friends represents a half chance for the appearance of mental problems.
But in order for a friendship to work, of course, it has to be achievable first.
In this communication through social media with an emphasis on the style and not the content of the message, an immediate response is often prioritized over meaningful contact.
Thanks to our digital lives, our need to look stronger has doubled, more fun-loving and more successful, even though we are rather isolated in reality.
This superficial success is dominated by what psychologists call external values; money, image, social status, everything at the expense of our real values - our personal desire for growth and friendship - which have a profound effect on our mental health.
And, as one billionaire and game-makers recently explained, money doesn't really buy more happiness.
The beauty of the Friends series was the source of this idea for him.
None of the characters were defined by their work, but by their personalities, who shone through friendly interaction.
Joey's humour and Chandler's frequent sacrifice, moments of watching football in comfortable armchairs, Ross's desire for Rachel, all stems from the recognition that yes, men can also identify with it, even though they mostly keep their feelings to themselves.
as a Samaritan worker explained to me: ..."my role as a listener of people who feel depressed or have suicidal tendencies has really confirmed to me that even the slightest friendly interactions can make a difference."
Men who contact us often feel the inability to communicate with friends.
The old adage "dealing with it" is strong in the sense that it will be seen as weak to open about problems.
Friends are one of the shows that arise in my memory when I try to offer a longer-term balancing mechanism.
It emphasizes small moments in male friendship.
When it comes to my task, the condition of an outsider on the phone helps people in moments of despair, but long-term support has to come from more closely, from a network of friends and family.
Therefore, if you are going to FrendsFest organized by Comedy Central this weekend, why not take your friends for a drink to Central Perk, where the interior will be completely redesigned to celebrate.
Or when you see someone alone at Starbucks, smile at him and say hello.
You never know the difference your friendship can make.
The Friends series will be broadcast on Comedy Central from start to finish - 236 episodes - for #FriendsFest
Di Maria scored and helped PSG sweep Malmo aside
Angel Di Maria celebrates with Blais Matuidi and Edinson Cavani after scoring a goal against Malma.
Angel Di Maria scored on his debut in the Champions League, helping PSG beat Sweden's Malmo team on Tuesday.
Here's a message about the match and the reaction to the game.
Angel Di Maria and Edinson Cavani both scored in Tuesday's game and Paris Saint-Germain began their journey in the Champions League with a final comfortable 2-0 victory against Malma at the Parc des Princes.
Di Maria scored the goal less than four minutes after his debut in the Champions League and this PSG player sent the French champions for victory, but then threw away a few chances until they finally increased their lead when Cavani scored the goal with his head in the 61st minute.
Real Madrid and Shakhtar Donetsk are also in Group A, which is which Paris needed a strong start against Swedish champions because they focus not only on qualifying for the knockout rounds, but on improving their trip to the quarter-finals they had recorded in each of the previous 3 seasons.
But even though they scored three points, it wasn’t the classic performance of Laurent Blanco’s team and Zlatan Ibrahimovic was vain in front of goal and was later replaced against his home team and the club in which he began his renowned career in 1999.
"It's not easy to win outside or at home in the Champions League, so a 2-0 win makes problems for our opponents and looking comfortable in defence is a good start, although there are things we'll have to improve with the progress of the season," Blanc said.
That's a good sign.
We wanted to win and we won, yet I regret that we didn't score one or two more wickets.
Ibrahimovic returned after an injury as one of three changes to the PSG team, who made a 2-2 draw at home this weekend with Bordeaux in League 1, but was overshadowed by Di Mariou in the fourth minute.
The Argentine wing has been labelled as a man who will take PSG in Europe to a new level after a transfer from Manchester United, which took place last month, and showed why, when he ran onto Marco Verratti's pass for Malmo's defence and, with a beautiful finish, sent the ball behind Johan Wilanda's back into the far corner of the goal.
However, if anyone expected it to kick-start PSG's spectacular victory, he had to be disappointed.
Malmo, who beat Celtic in the play-offs and got to that stage, came on with 9 internationals in the starting line-up and five defenders, and the two overcaught midfielders were a tough opponent.
But in addition to Nikola Djurdjice's shot, who only narrowly missed Kevin Trapp's far-foot corner in the 34th minute didn't offer much in attack.
Instead, PSG themselves deprived themselves of benefits from the attack with bad passes and a miserable finish that wasn't good even from Ibrahimovic, who couldn't shot even five times in the first half, and was then disappointed because his shot arrived in the net shortly after the start of the second half of Cavani.
However, he proved much more appropriate when it came to preparing chances for his team-mates and it was just from his pass, when Di Maria sentered a torque shot that ended in Wilande's goal after 52 minutes.
The second goal finally came after an hour of play thanks to Cavani, this Uruguayan's header sent behind the line his sixth goal this season after Ibrahimovic passed to him after a pass from Maxwell's left wing.
That ended Malmo's thoughts about the possibility that they could make anything out of the match, and only an exquisite intervention by Wiland from very closes thwarted David Luiz's hopes and the score remained unchanged even when Ezequiel Lavezzi's goal was unrecognised at the end of the match.
Malmo Age Hareide's coach later admitted that PSG were a class higher above his team, the Norwegian said: "Our last game in the Champions League took place in December last year."
We've only played the Swedish league since then, which isn't on the same level.
Qualifying matches aren't enough.
Hat down for Paris.
For them, it's been a breathtaking match.
Glencore earned $2.5 billion for selling shares
Mining giant Glencore, based in Switzerland, which has been hitted by the collapse of commodity prices, made £$2.5 billion from the sale of shares on Wednesday as part of its large-scale debt reduction plan.
Glencore said in a statement that it had sold new shares worth about £1.6 billion to repay the debt.
The company, which has lost 57 percent of its market value this year, is grappling with commodity prices, as China's slowdown in the economy reduces demand and ignites sparks of destruction across markets.
The rights issue sent Glencore's stock prices to the top spot on London's stock market in the early-morning deals on Wednesday.
Shares jumped 2.77 percent to 131.60 pence on the FTSE 100 index, which opened 0.69 percent higher.
Glencore revealed last week that a $2.5-billion stock sale is part of broader plans to reduce their $30 billion debt, which has accumulated by about a third
The company sold new shares at a price of 123 pence per share, marking a 2.4 percent discount on the closing price on Tuesday.
They disposed of 1.3 billion shares at a total price of 9.99 percent per group.
Fears of prolonging China's brakes have reduced the price of iron ore by almost half and coal, with copper and other commodities falling by 20 to 40 percent.
The White House confirmed that Washington was visited by Xi Jinping
Chinese President Xi Jinping will visit the U.S. at the end of the month, the White House confirmed in its Tuesday report.
President Xi's first visit to Washington will take place on September 25 in Barack Obama's response to a visit to Beijing, where the president visited in November, the report said.
U.S. President and First Lady Michelle Obama will invite President Xi and his wife Peng Liyuan to a state dinner that will take place after the talks are over.
“President Xi’s visit represents an opportunity to strengthen U.S.-China’s mutual relations, urging cooperation in resolving problems in the field of international, regional and common interests, and is an opportunity for constructive debate about the disagreements between the two states.”
Xi, whose visit was first announced in February, is expected to visit Seattle, where he will meet with several U.S. technical leaders on his way to Washington, and then continue to New York for a September U.N. meeting.
Obama and Xi are expected to address contentious issues, including Washington's fears of China's alleged cyber-espionage and its growing aggressiveness in South Chinese Sea waters.
How the refugee crisis has shattered the Schengen area.
The emergency flare detonated by Germany has far failed the European crew, but is already forcing the ship to change course, French daily Le Figaro wrote.
On Monday, the European Union decided to deal first with the problem of refugees: it would rather systematically sort migrants and asylum seekers as soon as they set foot on the old continent - especially in Greece, rather than arguing over the future distribution of refugees according to quotas based on an amount that is increasingly moving away from reality.
Six days after European Commission President Jean-Claude Juncker asked 25 of the 28 EU countries to redistribute 160,000 refugees, the interior ministers of the Member States changed their priority.
Rather than taking care of the final destination of refugees, it is first necessary to filter those who are already arriving today and solve the fate of those who are already moving within the Schengen area.
"Effective management of the EU's external borders is essential for coping with migration," the text discussed by ministers said in Brussels on Monday.
After the crisis of the common currency, the EU discovers that it is at risk in its next major integration project.
Germany may be surprised, but it is not losing its decisiveness and increasing pressure.
It restores border controls, especially with Austria, but without blocking the influx of those seeking asylum in that country.
In doing so, the central power makes it clear that no free movement in Europe is possible without it.
It has already served as evidence that Austria and Slovakia joined this measure on Monday.
Poland, the Netherlands, Belgium and even France are also considering it, albeit with varying intensities.
Hungary approached this for a long time.
These exceptional and temporary measures are permissible under Schengen rules.
That it's a domino effect, but he also sees the blind.
The key issue for Berlin and Paris is now the effective introduction of mandatory entry controls in Greece, in southern Italy, as well as in Hungary for those who have already passed inland.
French Interior Minister Bernard Cazeneuve and his German colleague Thomas de Maizière discussed this intensively during a private conversation with their three colleagues from Greece, Italy and Hungary.
This idea is not new, but it is solidified.
According to unofficial statistics, two-thirds of newly arrived refugees were already registered and identified by fingerprinting.
Budapest refuses the introduction of any entry point on its territory, because it fears more than others, so as not to leave on its shoulders the camps in which refugees would accumulate at its expense.
Greece remains a true black hole.
More than 200 thousand people have landed on its islands near the Turkish coast this year, which is twice as many as have recently arrived in Italy.
Most are true asylum seekers who came directly from Syria after going through overcrowded camps in Lebanon, Turkey and Jordan.
It's a huge gaping opening.
Athens, which is still in financial disruption and waiting for a new government, has folded their hands into their lines.
That's the starting point for the Balkan route and a source now of common concerns for German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbana.
Juncker's commission promises to introduce checkpoints as soon as possible, but gave no number or date.
"The challenges faced by Greece apply to the whole of Europe," EU interior ministers agreed on Monday to mobilise on that front.
It is therefore a matter of deploying in this country the intervention teams of the European border control agency Frontex.
The EU wants to help Greece expand its capacity to receive asylum seekers and strengthen surveillance of the islands.
"Appropriate financial resources will be needed," warns the text submitted to ministers for approval.
From the point of view of the law, Athens will continue to have the final say.
The question of the emergence of real European border guards has not been officially raised, but Europe is approaching it.
Either way, the twenty-eight's new emergency has postponed the issue of binding quotas and 160,000 refugees raised earlier this year by the Juncker Commission and the European Parliament and strongly supported by Angela Merkel and French President François Hollande.
Hungary, Slovakia, the Czech Republic and Poland still don't even want to hear about it.
They consider it an attack on their sovereignty.
The number, but not the obligation, was retained on Monday in a text approved mostly by twenty-eight.
But as a political commitment.
In other words, the process is interrupted.
The only firm promise includes the redistribution of 40,000 refugees over the next two years, which has already been reached an agreement in June during a hectic EU summit.
Seven dead under an avalanche in the Alps, of which reportedly three Czechs.
Three Czechs and four Germans are among the seven tourists killed by an avalanche in the French Alps.
According to the authorities, German nationality is also a woman who was the only one of those affected by the eight-member group.
Previous media reports said it was about Poland.
A thirty-year-old woman has been hospitalized in Briancon with a serious femur injury.
According to AFP, the dead consisted mostly of forties.
"The rescue operation is over, the bodies of the victims have also been transported to Briancon for identification," Pierre Besnard, prefect of the Hautes-Alpes department, clarified.
The accident happened around noon in the Pelvoux mountain area below the summit of Mount Dôme de Neige des Ecrins measuring 4015 metres.
"The sudden avalanche was 250 meters wide and about a meter high," Nicolas Colombani, the chief of paramedics, noted.
According to him, an enormous amount of snow rushed to the eight-member group at a height of about 3900 meters.
Today, the weather service did not warn of bad weather in the area.
Trpišovský before the game with Braga: Finally the right Europe.
Coach Jindřich Trpišovský was able to go through the preliminary rounds of the Europa League to the basic group during his première in the cups and now he is looking forward to enjoying the right Europe with the team.
At the beginning of the main stage of the competition, Slovan welcomes the finalist from 2011 Braga and, according to the coach, wants to impose on her playing style.
After all the nerves in the knockout fights, when every goal and every situation could knock you out on your way to the dream group, now we're there and we're going to play six games.
