Xi calls for an opening up of the economy to the wider world
It is necessary to open the economy to the wider world to boost growth, President Xi Jinping told a group whose task is Tuesday's governance reform.
"China should engage in attracting foreign investment and expertise and improving open policies," he said regarding the 16th meeting of the Central Head of the Group on Deepening the Overall Reform.
Promoting the opening of emerging reforms will add new momentum and vitality, it will also provide new space for economic growth, according to Xi.
The leading group has adopted a number of directives, including a negative list of regulatory access to the market, loosening of border control policies, support for state-owned enterprises (SOEs) to absorb private capital and reorganisation of the registration resident system.
The statement said a negative list will be drawn up identifying sectors and businesses that are beyond investment limits.
This system will be gradually tested and improved through test programs.
The move is significant because it will give the market a greater role in allocating resources, ensuring a legal business environment and making the market more open, the statement said.
The government will also release controls on the powers it delegates to companies, making sure that the firms themselves decide how to manage their businesses.
The statement also states that China will allow border areas to discover new models of cross-border economic cooperation and new mechanisms for promoting regional growth.
In an effort to open the state-controlled sector, more areas will be open to private investment.
"The government will not change its policy towards foreign investment and will protect the legal interests of companies that are financed from abroad and will provide them with better services," the statement said.
Moreover, it will be easier for foreigners to apply for a permanent residence permit or "green card," thanks to optimizing the requirements and simplifying the application process.
Ensuring the rights of lawyers to practice and develop professional judges and prosecutors was also discussed at the meeting.
The meeting was also attended by Prime Minister Li Keqiang and leaders Liu Yunshan and Zhang Gaoli, according to a statement issued after the meeting.
New inpatient wards may be established in the Karlovy Vary Region.
According to the Deputy Governor of the Karlovy Vary Region Jakub Panik (ČSSD), the current capacity of inpatient departments of two regional hospitals and two private hospitals in the region is sufficient and partial adjustments should be made on the basis of an agreement between the departments of individual hospitals.
There are also opportunities to expand care in the county.
For example, in the department of surgical departments, one-day surgery centers could be created, where according to the established concept, procedures of surgical disciplines would be carried out, which do not require hospitalization for more than 24 hours.
It is not yet clear in which specific hospitals workplaces focused on one-day surgery should be.
The skin department of the Karlovy Vary hospital could create a laser center with the possibility of using laser technology for skin diseases.
The concept also recommended the formation of an Apalic Center, which would look after patients with brain involvement who are in a vegetative state.
Such a center should be established centrally and should be an integral part of the anesthesiology-resuscitation department.
There is also the possibility of concentrating such a facility at the Island Hospital, where similar care already exists.
Another inpatient capacity, which appears to be insufficient in the Karlovy Vary Region, are follow-up care beds, especially long-term care (LDN), whose capacity in Nejdek is insufficient and, above all, should be closely connected to acute care beds.
Part of this facility should act as rehabilitation beds and part as classic LDN beds, recommends the concept.
As he points out, after-care beds are missing mainly in the Karlovy Vary hospital.
That's where you could use abandoned buildings after centralization.
Last but not least in the Karlovy Vary region, social health beds with a focus on the treatment of Alzheimer's disease are absent.
There is a shortage of these beds throughout the country, so it could also serve for other regions as the so-called Alzheimer Center.
According to the recommendations, hospitals should also prepare for the introduction of so-called follow-up beds, for which funds of European subsidies will be released.
"These are beds that will build on the fields of supe-specialized care and will form a space for the translation of patients after the procedure, for example after cardiac surgery, neurosurgical, transplantation or other intervention," describes the regional concept of inpatient care.
New services could also be offered in psychiatric care.
